WEBVTT

1
00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:38.960
One face.

2
00:00:41.509 --> 00:00:42.678
One fleet.

3
00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:46.474
One war...

4
00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.281
One man...

5
00:00:54.483 --> 00:00:55.521
Just an idea...

6
00:00:59.362 --> 00:01:00.600
One trick...

7
00:01:03.161 --> 00:01:05.408
The ruse that will bring down the walls of Troy.

8
00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:10.725
And amid a deafening roar.
it will burn her to ashes!

9
00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:03.800
What do you mean?

10
00:02:12.086 --> 00:02:13.086
It's a gift.

11
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:17.500
For Athena.

12
00:02:20.781 --> 00:02:23.940
My people. pay homage
to the gods through their sacrifices.

13
00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.060
Pull him out of the water!

14
00:02:30.061 --> 00:02:31.480
Take him to the Temple of Athena.

15
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.160
The war is over.

16
00:02:55.832 --> 00:02:56.878
Stop it!

17
00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:02.360
Not this song.

18
00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.540
I want to remind them
whose wine they're drinking.

19
00:03:16.541 --> 00:03:19.840
I don't want a song about Odysseus.
I want Odysseus himself.

20
00:03:19.841 --> 00:03:23.048
So why do you
welcome them into his home every evening?

21
00:03:23.441 --> 00:03:27.422
In this world. men do whatever they want.

22
00:03:27.641 --> 00:03:29.040
And women are doing what they can.

23
00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:31.006
I'm a man now. Mom.

24
00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:33.120
Then kick them out.

25
00:03:34.394 --> 00:03:37.860
They're breaking Dia's law.
All it takes is an excuse. and they'll attack you.

26
00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.525
I've already lost my husband.

27
00:03:46.061 --> 00:03:47.395
I don't want to lose my son. too.

28
00:03:47.801 --> 00:03:49.000
My father will come back.

29
00:03:49.001 --> 00:03:50.325
So where is he?

30
00:03:50.801 --> 00:03:53.640
Eight years have passed since the fall of Troy.

31
00:03:53.641 --> 00:03:55.540
And it's been almost twenty years since he left.

32
00:03:55.541 --> 00:03:56.541
Where is it?

33
00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.860
We must welcome him just
as we welcome each one of you.

34
00:04:10.861 --> 00:04:12.120
Are you calling us beggars?

35
00:04:12.121 --> 00:04:14.538
I'm saying you've been guests here for three years now.

36
00:04:15.061 --> 00:04:17.700
Soon we'll be relying
on your hospitality.

37
00:04:19.599 --> 00:04:20.945
Because your mother can't make up her mind.

38
00:04:21.574 --> 00:04:23.073
She's already married.

39
00:04:23.201 --> 00:04:24.201
Odysseus is dead.

40
00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:26.700
And what do you know about my father?

41
00:04:29.515 --> 00:04:31.969
I know he left you
nothing but flocks and old wine.

42
00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:34.725
You managed to miss the best part
during the first two years.

43
00:04:35.047 --> 00:04:36.613
And I know he never came home.

44
00:04:37.011 --> 00:04:37.707
Not yet.

45
00:04:39.524 --> 00:04:42.332
You're still clinging to your father.
whom you never even knew.

46
00:04:43.455 --> 00:04:45.054
What a pathetic kid.

47
00:04:45.476 --> 00:04:48.156
Since none of us can know.

48
00:04:48.188 --> 00:04:49.408
Whoever his father is.
don't claim to know mine.

49
00:04:49.987 --> 00:04:51.072
But I knew him.

50
00:04:51.612 --> 00:04:53.776
When we were little. we used to watch him on his hunting trips.

51
00:04:55.606 --> 00:04:57.740
I volunteered
to join his expedition to Troy.

52
00:04:57.741 --> 00:05:01.113
He asked me to stay here
and keep an eye on you.

53
00:05:01.584 --> 00:05:02.683
At me or at my mother?

54
00:05:04.628 --> 00:05:06.045
You can't blame me for my happiness.

55
00:05:06.129 --> 00:05:07.825
My father will come back.

56
00:05:07.909 --> 00:05:10.466
And then his hospitality
won't be like hers.

57
00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:12.140
So would he be breaking Dia's law?

58
00:05:12.687 --> 00:05:15.115
God's law commands us
to treat strangers in such a way that

59
00:05:15.178 --> 00:05:17.880
just as we would like them to treat us.
They might be gods in disguise.

60
00:05:18.531 --> 00:05:21.049
I think we can agree. Antinous.
that you are definitely not a god.

61
00:05:22.421 --> 00:05:24.361
And this one definitely doesn't smell like a god.

62
00:05:24.393 --> 00:05:26.172
If he were. his disguise
wouldn't be very convincing.

63
00:05:26.372 --> 00:05:30.080
Be careful—
he might strike you down with lightning right on the spot.

64
00:05:43.916 --> 00:05:45.575
That thunder was just a coincidence.

65
00:05:46.626 --> 00:05:47.620
I don't know.

66
00:05:48.132 --> 00:05:51.557
You have wise eyes…
eyes like Athena's.

67
00:05:51.641 --> 00:05:53.440
Look. do you see all those traitors?

68
00:05:54.301 --> 00:05:55.592
From Sparta.

69
00:05:55.676 --> 00:05:57.515
Has Menelaus returned from the war?

70
00:05:58.473 --> 00:05:59.473
Years ago.

71
00:05:59.557 --> 00:06:01.178
But Odysseus never returned.

72
00:06:01.455 --> 00:06:03.100
We don't even know if he's still alive.

73
00:06:03.101 --> 00:06:03.921
Did you hear anything?

74
00:06:04.005 --> 00:06:05.944
Songs about his victory in the war.

75
00:06:06.028 --> 00:06:07.000
We've all heard those.

76
00:06:07.001 --> 00:06:07.613
Anything else?

77
00:06:07.697 --> 00:06:08.697
No.

78
00:06:09.865 --> 00:06:14.234
But Sparta is full of old warriors
from Troy.

79
00:06:14.318 --> 00:06:16.317
Someone must know something.

80
00:06:16.401 --> 00:06:17.955
Are you Odysseus's son?

81
00:06:18.353 --> 00:06:19.492
Go to Sparta.

82
00:06:20.305 --> 00:06:22.380
Go see Menelaus.

83
00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:24.290
He'll welcome you.

84
00:06:26.356 --> 00:06:28.021
The queen wants to speak with the stranger.

85
00:06:29.953 --> 00:06:31.133
He lives only for the news.

86
00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:36.153
Just don't try to
give her false hope.

87
00:06:36.237 --> 00:06:39.671
He has his own way of finding out
whether anyone has actually seen *The Odyssey*.

88
00:06:39.755 --> 00:06:40.994
Do you see that man?

89
00:06:41.078 --> 00:06:43.952
He doesn't see very well anymore.
but that doesn't stop him

90
00:06:44.036 --> 00:06:47.200
to kill the pilgrims for the lies
they spread about my father.

91
00:07:14.475 --> 00:07:16.289
Slow down. slow down—it's too fast.

92
00:07:16.821 --> 00:07:18.080
And how did you know that?

93
00:07:18.164 --> 00:07:21.050
I don't need eyes
to hear your hurried heartbeat.

94
00:07:21.741 --> 00:07:23.080
Mentor. put some pressure on him.

95
00:07:31.426 --> 00:07:32.586
Too fast.

96
00:07:42.025 --> 00:07:43.360
He's just as friendly as you are.

97
00:07:44.213 --> 00:07:45.956
He only listened to your father.

98
00:07:46.614 --> 00:07:47.915
Where did he bring him from?

99
00:07:48.568 --> 00:07:49.568
He found him here.

100
00:07:59.291 --> 00:08:00.122
I'll take it.

101
00:08:00.441 --> 00:08:01.640
For your son?

102
00:08:01.724 --> 00:08:02.724
Like a hunting dog.

103
00:08:04.945 --> 00:08:06.380
Damn it. it looks like he was right.

104
00:08:41.588 --> 00:08:42.610
Stop!

105
00:08:42.694 --> 00:08:44.344
How deep do you want the cut to be?

106
00:08:45.918 --> 00:08:47.666
Why do you always push people's buttons like that?

107
00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:49.099
It's a matter of honor.

108
00:08:49.183 --> 00:08:50.481
To warn your prey?

109
00:08:51.201 --> 00:08:52.340
So that she may fight with honor.

110
00:08:52.341 --> 00:08:53.660
You weren't fighting; you were hunting.

111
00:08:53.834 --> 00:08:56.113
Hunting cannot be compared
to a battle where cunning is the deciding factor.

112
00:08:56.494 --> 00:09:00.095
Cunning. cunning…
but that cunning will come back to haunt you one day.

113
00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:01.859
Try again.

114
00:09:02.121 --> 00:09:04.637
But this time.
don't be too hasty or too precise.

115
00:09:04.721 --> 00:09:07.076
You don't win with perfect defense.

116
00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:08.379
Provoke an attack.

117
00:09:08.463 --> 00:09:09.200
How?

118
00:09:09.284 --> 00:09:10.643
Hesitate.

119
00:09:10.727 --> 00:09:12.966
Leave his stomach exposed.

120
00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:15.289
That way. his attack
will become your opportunity.

121
00:09:46.471 --> 00:09:47.857
Is this what you taught my father?

122
00:09:47.941 --> 00:09:50.172
No. no. he's the one who taught me.

123
00:09:50.901 --> 00:09:54.140
He said. “You won’t always be the strongest.”

124
00:09:54.214 --> 00:09:57.123
"Not even the fastest.
so be the smartest."

125
00:09:57.775 --> 00:09:59.103
What do you think he would do?

126
00:09:59.507 --> 00:10:02.006
What if he came back and found
all those suitors in the house?

127
00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:05.412
I can't even imagine
what he would do to them.

128
00:10:05.496 --> 00:10:07.155
There are a lot of them.

129
00:10:07.239 --> 00:10:09.240
Get rid of two.
and the rest will fall apart.

130
00:10:09.324 --> 00:10:10.806
They're just a bunch of blowhards and cowards.

131
00:10:11.654 --> 00:10:13.354
Most of them were old enough to go

132
00:10:13.438 --> 00:10:18.238
to Troy. but when they saw Agamemnon’s
sails. their fathers hid them.

133
00:10:18.593 --> 00:10:19.893
Their fathers hid them.

134
00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:25.638
And did you know
that Agamemnon's war

135
00:10:25.838 --> 00:10:27.700
It will take years
and claim many lives.

136
00:10:32.823 --> 00:10:34.853
Therefore. he assembled his army by drawing lots.

137
00:10:37.604 --> 00:10:38.619
Name?

138
00:10:38.721 --> 00:10:39.540
Sinon.

139
00:10:39.541 --> 00:10:40.380
Pull.

140
00:10:40.381 --> 00:10:41.220
No.

141
00:10:41.221 --> 00:10:42.040
Name?

142
00:10:42.041 --> 00:10:42.880
Antinous.

143
00:10:42.881 --> 00:10:43.881
Pull.

144
00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:45.800
Yes.

145
00:10:45.884 --> 00:10:46.884
By drawing lots?

146
00:10:49.466 --> 00:10:51.466
Antinous told me
that he had volunteered.

147
00:11:01.693 --> 00:11:04.067
One boy wants to take Sinon's place.

148
00:11:04.587 --> 00:11:05.786
I won't let that happen.

149
00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:08.169
He has to stay here
and take care of his family.

150
00:11:11.195 --> 00:11:12.880
You. too. can take care of your son.

151
00:11:12.881 --> 00:11:13.881
Of course.

152
00:11:17.857 --> 00:11:18.857
He's with me.

153
00:11:19.705 --> 00:11:21.521
Is this what Odysseus wanted. Antinous—
for you to keep an eye on me?

154
00:11:21.605 --> 00:11:22.694
No. no. no.

155
00:11:22.927 --> 00:11:25.806
But Odysseus
understood his fathers' shame.

156
00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:27.609
He felt sorry for those fathers.

157
00:11:27.693 --> 00:11:31.232
You and your mother were
more important to him than the promise of fame.

158
00:11:32.465 --> 00:11:34.160
So he knew it would take years.

159
00:11:35.104 --> 00:11:36.415
Did he tell my mother that?

160
00:11:36.674 --> 00:11:38.233
That's a question for her.

161
00:11:38.317 --> 00:11:41.256
You might be wondering
if he'll ever come back at all.

162
00:11:43.105 --> 00:11:44.428
Argos believes that.

163
00:11:44.512 --> 00:11:46.351
I've never seen a dog
live to be twenty years old.

164
00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:49.780
And so he outlived his master.

165
00:12:06.428 --> 00:12:07.694
I didn't mean to interrupt you.

166
00:12:11.115 --> 00:12:12.300
I'm in no hurry.

167
00:12:13.705 --> 00:12:16.407
The elders decided
that once I finish

168
00:12:16.408 --> 00:12:18.540
Weaving a burial shroud for your grandfather—
it will be time to set out again.

169
00:12:18.624 --> 00:12:19.624
Why?

170
00:12:25.013 --> 00:12:27.693
Ithaca had been without a king for too long.

171
00:12:27.777 --> 00:12:29.496
They want safety.

172
00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:30.619
How long ago?

173
00:12:30.703 --> 00:12:32.167
Before the Sea Nations.

174
00:12:32.251 --> 00:12:34.690
These are just stories told by pilgrims.

175
00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:36.799
Everything we know about the world
comes from the stories of travelers.

176
00:12:36.961 --> 00:12:38.672
That's how we learned that Troy had fallen.

177
00:12:39.114 --> 00:12:42.213
Now everyone is telling stories
about the raids by the Sea Peoples.

178
00:12:43.613 --> 00:12:45.483
Who are the Sea Peoples?

179
00:12:45.754 --> 00:12:48.593
No one knows. but the elders wanted
us to be prepared.

180
00:12:48.677 --> 00:12:50.076
Ready?

181
00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:52.459
We are part of the greatest civilization
the world has ever known.

182
00:12:54.406 --> 00:12:56.186
Look at this huge palace.

183
00:12:56.421 --> 00:13:01.324
Stones as old as time itself. left at the mercy
of greedy. unwelcome guests.

184
00:13:02.361 --> 00:13:03.720
This building is worthless

185
00:13:04.569 --> 00:13:07.059
without people's respect
for her legacy.

186
00:13:07.143 --> 00:13:08.202
The Law of Dia.

187
00:13:09.126 --> 00:13:11.079
Treat others the way
you want them to treat you.

188
00:13:11.549 --> 00:13:13.828
And when they abuse it. they destroy everything.

189
00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:17.260
How did you even come up with the idea
to marry one of them?

190
00:13:17.344 --> 00:13:19.083
Because your father asked me to

191
00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:22.567
a long time ago.

192
00:13:22.781 --> 00:13:25.060
Agamemnon is our king.

193
00:13:25.144 --> 00:13:26.963
The Trojans took his brother's wife.

194
00:13:27.047 --> 00:13:29.246
Or perhaps she left with him on her own.

195
00:13:29.441 --> 00:13:32.860
In any case. the walls of Troy
are said to be impregnable.

196
00:13:34.085 --> 00:13:38.260
We'll stay on the outskirts of the big city
and try to lure them out into battle.

197
00:13:40.575 --> 00:13:43.806
As the years go by.
Telemachus will grow up

198
00:13:44.006 --> 00:13:46.540
and you'll get used
to life without me.

199
00:13:46.541 --> 00:13:48.600
Menelaus will give up Helen.

200
00:13:53.348 --> 00:13:55.914
It's just an excuse for Agamemnon
to gain

201
00:13:55.998 --> 00:13:58.137
Troy's control
over the trade routes.

202
00:14:00.517 --> 00:14:01.992
He won't let it go.

203
00:14:02.751 --> 00:14:03.751
Never.

204
00:14:11.293 --> 00:14:12.593
What if you refuse?

205
00:14:18.528 --> 00:14:19.793
She's going to marry our son.

206
00:14:21.817 --> 00:14:23.259
He won't do that.

207
00:14:23.601 --> 00:14:26.980
He sacrificed his daughter
to ensure a favorable wind.

208
00:14:28.505 --> 00:14:30.790
Why would the gods want
his daughter's life?

209
00:14:31.421 --> 00:14:33.240
The Value of Sacrifice

210
00:14:34.845 --> 00:14:37.180
lies in the price
paid by the one who brings it.

211
00:14:40.945 --> 00:14:42.580
That's terrible.

212
00:14:44.995 --> 00:14:46.120
And he did it.

213
00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:53.060
I won't be back anytime soon.
if I ever come back at all.

214
00:14:57.890 --> 00:14:58.940
What if we ran away?

215
00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:04.346
On your fastest ship.
with your best men.

216
00:15:04.637 --> 00:15:06.707
and would we disappear over the horizon?

217
00:15:08.380 --> 00:15:14.560
Holding hands on deck.
rowing into the sunset…

218
00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:20.160
There is a world beyond these walls as well.

219
00:15:21.095 --> 00:15:22.960
But Agamemnon's Revenge

220
00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:26.540
would weigh down on all of Ithaca.

221
00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:34.000
Promise me.

222
00:15:36.239 --> 00:15:42.041
If I don't return. and when Telemachus comes of age.
promise me.

223
00:15:43.929 --> 00:15:45.420
that you're going to marry someone else.

224
00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:49.460
There have been enough victims already.

225
00:15:49.461 --> 00:15:51.160
Love is not a sacrifice.

226
00:15:51.161 --> 00:15:52.161
It is.

227
00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:55.000
And I don't want you to bring it.

228
00:15:55.001 --> 00:15:56.100
It's not up to you to decide.

229
00:15:58.975 --> 00:15:59.975
Promise me.

230
00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:02.700
No.

231
00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:08.180
Promise me you'll come back.

232
00:16:13.815 --> 00:16:14.940
What if I can't?

233
00:16:21.095 --> 00:16:23.760
I won't live on just memories of you.

234
00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:29.820
Take my brooch.

235
00:16:30.695 --> 00:16:32.460
Always wear it.

236
00:16:32.461 --> 00:16:34.640
Athena will bring you home.

237
00:16:34.641 --> 00:16:36.000
I don't think it suits me.

238
00:16:39.041 --> 00:16:41.853
I will ask every pilgrim who claims
to have seen you.

239
00:16:41.854 --> 00:16:45.880
what you were wearing.
and I'll know if he's lying.

240
00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:50.220
If the Sea Peoples really existed.

241
00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:55.060
Then Ithaca is without a king…
and faces an army of suitors.

242
00:16:55.870 --> 00:16:57.220
I can keep this hall in order.

243
00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:00.720
Come on.

244
00:17:12.906 --> 00:17:13.436
Draw the bow.

245
00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:25.367
That's not fair.

246
00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:27.740
You said that
only your father could make him get worked up.

247
00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:29.420
He was happy to let others try it. too.

248
00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:33.920
And he lined up all the axes.

249
00:18:06.204 --> 00:18:07.620
And no one else has ever managed to do it.

250
00:18:09.044 --> 00:18:11.204
Mom. they're wearing you out.
forcing you to choose

251
00:18:11.205 --> 00:18:14.219
before I can
lay claim to the throne.

252
00:18:14.861 --> 00:18:17.960
The stranger said
that Menelaus had returned years ago.

253
00:18:17.961 --> 00:18:18.900
They all came back.

254
00:18:18.901 --> 00:18:20.300
The survivors.

255
00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:23.880
Maybe he died.

256
00:18:23.881 --> 00:18:25.980
And if he's dead.
this palace is mine.

257
00:18:27.174 --> 00:18:28.440
We need to find out the truth.

258
00:18:28.441 --> 00:18:31.436
I have to go see Menelaus
to find out the truth.

259
00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:34.780
It's not because these suitors
pose a threat to you.

260
00:18:34.781 --> 00:18:36.405
Do you think I don't know that?

261
00:18:37.181 --> 00:18:39.820
Every night they pressure me
to give them the excuse they're waiting for.

262
00:18:39.821 --> 00:18:42.700
Whether at sea or on the road.

263
00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:44.820
They won't need any excuse.

264
00:18:44.821 --> 00:18:46.100
You won't come back.

265
00:18:48.099 --> 00:18:50.460
You don't want me to lay claim to the throne.

266
00:18:52.174 --> 00:18:56.000
You want me to stay in the background.
just so you can remain the queen of Ithaca.

267
00:18:56.001 --> 00:18:59.240
I'm tired of being the queen of Ithaca
without my king.

268
00:18:59.241 --> 00:19:00.400
Without their king?

269
00:19:01.964 --> 00:19:03.080
Or just without a king?

270
00:19:04.504 --> 00:19:07.380
And do you think you're ready
to take your father's place?

271
00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:10.260
You're still a kid.

272
00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:23.000
Lower your voice. Melanthius.

273
00:19:23.001 --> 00:19:24.840
Don't step on my bruises.

274
00:19:24.841 --> 00:19:27.000
You're a shepherd. not a rider.

275
00:19:27.001 --> 00:19:30.880
And you're just a swineherd.
Who do you think you are. talking to me like that?

276
00:19:30.881 --> 00:19:34.080
I am a servant to whom my master
has entrusted his flocks.

277
00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:38.380
Your master has been gone for years. you silly old man.

278
00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:42.220
And your presence here is just as
unwelcome as this dog's.

279
00:19:42.221 --> 00:19:43.221
Argos!

280
00:19:46.760 --> 00:19:47.760
Argos!

281
00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:51.521
Argos!

282
00:19:53.099 --> 00:19:55.200
Sir. he didn't do anything.

283
00:19:55.201 --> 00:19:56.340
So that stench is coming from you?

284
00:19:57.600 --> 00:19:59.400
Leave that old dog alone.

285
00:19:59.401 --> 00:20:00.240
Who?

286
00:20:00.241 --> 00:20:01.241
Both of them.

287
00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:03.400
What did you say. slave?

288
00:20:03.401 --> 00:20:04.680
I got carried away.

289
00:20:04.681 --> 00:20:06.380
Argos is my father's hunting dog.

290
00:20:06.381 --> 00:20:07.381
His dog?

291
00:20:11.621 --> 00:20:12.621
Take a look at him.

292
00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:15.480
Take him away; we're trying to eat.

293
00:20:15.481 --> 00:20:17.720
No. can't you see that he's suffering?

294
00:20:17.721 --> 00:20:19.160
Okay.

295
00:20:19.161 --> 00:20:20.600
I'll spare him the suffering.

296
00:20:20.601 --> 00:20:21.820
Leave him alone!

297
00:20:39.497 --> 00:20:40.837
Did you see that?

298
00:20:41.561 --> 00:20:42.561
Did you all see that?

299
00:20:44.246 --> 00:20:46.086
He attacked me!

300
00:20:46.421 --> 00:20:48.180
And he had every right to do so!

301
00:20:48.181 --> 00:20:49.181
Please!

302
00:20:51.394 --> 00:20:52.960
Please. don't hurt my son.

303
00:20:56.174 --> 00:20:57.260
Calm down. calm down.

304
00:20:57.261 --> 00:20:58.760
Everyone. please calm down.

305
00:20:58.761 --> 00:20:59.800
The boy got carried away.

306
00:20:59.801 --> 00:21:00.801
Have a little compassion.

307
00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:04.600
And have another glass of wine.

308
00:21:04.601 --> 00:21:05.640
I'll take care of it.

309
00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:08.340
It's my father's hunting dog.

310
00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:11.940
And he survived to wait for Odysseus.

311
00:21:11.941 --> 00:21:14.340
Okay. I'll put it
on the compost pile by the entrance.

312
00:21:14.341 --> 00:21:16.580
so that he could welcome his master a little earlier.

313
00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:28.360
That dog is staying outside!

314
00:21:30.260 --> 00:21:32.841
Next time. think carefully
about whether you're willing to risk your life for an animal.

315
00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:45.740
You're hurt.

316
00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:50.620
Get the supplies ready. take them to the ships.
and don't tell anyone.

317
00:21:50.621 --> 00:21:52.060
Take only those you trust.

318
00:21:52.061 --> 00:21:53.820
You're going to break your mother's heart.

319
00:21:53.821 --> 00:21:55.980
If I stay. they'll force me to fight.

320
00:21:55.981 --> 00:21:57.320
And no one will take my side.

321
00:21:57.321 --> 00:21:59.260
I have to find out what happened to my father.

322
00:21:59.261 --> 00:22:02.900
The people of Ithaca will support me
if they find out that Odysseus is dead.

323
00:22:02.901 --> 00:22:04.500
But the suitors will never allow it.

324
00:22:04.501 --> 00:22:05.900
You won't come back alive.

325
00:22:05.901 --> 00:22:09.377
Tell the Mentor to meet me
by the boats before dawn.

326
00:22:09.701 --> 00:22:12.739
And what should I tell your mother?

327
00:22:13.247 --> 00:22:15.106
May he hold out as long as he can.

328
00:22:18.820 --> 00:22:20.540
The ship is ready.
and the tide is favorable.

329
00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:24.020
You never told me
that you have wise eyes?

330
00:22:24.021 --> 00:22:25.297
How is Athena doing?

331
00:22:25.621 --> 00:22:26.820
Once or twice.

332
00:22:26.821 --> 00:22:28.582
You haven't asked me yet where we're going.

333
00:22:28.666 --> 00:22:30.265
Where are we going?

334
00:22:30.349 --> 00:22:31.722
Find my father.

335
00:22:32.161 --> 00:22:34.560
It's alive.
I can feel it in the wind and in the waves.

336
00:22:34.561 --> 00:22:37.760
He's out there somewhere.
lost. hurt.

337
00:22:37.761 --> 00:22:40.300
I don't know where.
but he's still alive.

338
00:23:12.121 --> 00:23:13.379
Those dreams again?

339
00:23:14.839 --> 00:23:17.880
Men who are drowning… inside.

340
00:23:19.026 --> 00:23:22.340
A dark place.

341
00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:27.440
The light from the fire reflects off the stone… I don't know.

342
00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:31.820
Don't you want to remember?

343
00:23:32.081 --> 00:23:34.520
Why don't the gods speak a language
we can understand?

344
00:23:37.520 --> 00:23:40.900
And who doesn't understand thunder. fire.

345
00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:44.332
a child's smile. a bountiful harvest?

346
00:23:44.801 --> 00:23:46.400
You're hiding from us.

347
00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:51.400
Look. who's moving that cloud?

348
00:23:52.600 --> 00:23:53.900
Who is gathering those birds?

349
00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:56.680
Who moves the water?

350
00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:00.320
Who were you talking to?

351
00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:04.807
Athena visited me again.

352
00:24:05.309 --> 00:24:09.869
It tells me to remember…
and to leave.

353
00:24:10.221 --> 00:24:11.600
And why would you leave?

354
00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:12.960
We have everything we need right here.

355
00:24:13.985 --> 00:24:16.000
How long have I been here. Kalypso?

356
00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:22.200
I have a home somewhere.

357
00:24:24.140 --> 00:24:26.580
And someone is waiting for me.

358
00:24:27.739 --> 00:24:29.480
And how do you know that?

359
00:24:29.481 --> 00:24:30.481
I don't know.

360
00:24:32.980 --> 00:24:33.980
I just feel it.

361
00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:52.160
Everything we have here
could last forever.

362
00:24:52.161 --> 00:24:54.000
Immortality is for the gods alone.

363
00:24:54.001 --> 00:24:55.001
Are you a goddess?

364
00:24:57.740 --> 00:25:01.920
You want to remember. but what if
those memories destroy you?

365
00:25:01.921 --> 00:25:03.040
Then my happiness wasn't real.

366
00:25:11.420 --> 00:25:12.980
That's enough. no more.

367
00:25:12.981 --> 00:25:14.320
But I love her.

368
00:25:14.321 --> 00:25:16.600
Stop eating that lotus already.

369
00:25:16.601 --> 00:25:18.120
Tell me… what do you remember?

370
00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:21.260
After Troy.

371
00:25:23.175 --> 00:25:24.175
Troy.

372
00:25:30.429 --> 00:25:32.300
We won the war.

373
00:25:32.301 --> 00:25:33.640
And then?

374
00:26:39.340 --> 00:26:41.240
Ten years on that cursed coast.

375
00:26:46.460 --> 00:26:47.460
We're going home.

376
00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:50.620
Remember those
we left behind.

377
00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:57.515
Do we have enough supplies?

378
00:26:57.861 --> 00:26:59.400
We took what we needed from Troy.

379
00:27:00.460 --> 00:27:02.300
One or two stops won't hurt us.

380
00:27:03.462 --> 00:27:07.467
Go for it!

381
00:27:07.836 --> 00:27:12.788
Get to work!

382
00:27:14.366 --> 00:27:15.468
Paddles down.

383
00:27:15.551 --> 00:27:17.551
Take a break!

384
00:27:17.919 --> 00:27:21.115
Oars down!

385
00:27:25.620 --> 00:27:26.660
Hoist the sails.

386
00:27:26.661 --> 00:27:28.020
Keep an eye on the southerly winds.

387
00:27:28.021 --> 00:27:29.021
Yes. sir.

388
00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:32.560
Agamemnon is sailing west
along a well-known supply route.

389
00:27:32.561 --> 00:27:34.060
And she has more men to feed.

390
00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:36.540
We'll find places to settle down.

391
00:27:36.541 --> 00:27:37.380
Are you sure?

392
00:27:37.381 --> 00:27:38.200
It's a risk.

393
00:27:38.201 --> 00:27:40.480
I followed Agamemnon for quite a while.

394
00:27:40.481 --> 00:27:43.359
With a little luck and favorable winds.
we'll see

395
00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:46.440
another world. and we'll return
before Agamemnon.

396
00:27:49.976 --> 00:27:51.236
Warning!

397
00:28:56.160 --> 00:28:57.860
Keep the constellation Ursa Major on your right.

398
00:29:07.960 --> 00:29:09.380
Odysseus. land!

399
00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:14.740
Can you see that?

400
00:29:14.741 --> 00:29:16.040
You lucky bastard.

401
00:29:16.980 --> 00:29:18.680
We only have our last few supplies left.

402
00:29:18.681 --> 00:29:21.080
Assemble the landing party.
and bring food and water.

403
00:29:21.081 --> 00:29:22.720
Everything we need for three weeks.

404
00:29:22.721 --> 00:29:24.180
What if they refuse?

405
00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:27.760
How much do you want to go home?

406
00:29:28.620 --> 00:29:30.520
Get your weapons ready.

407
00:29:30.521 --> 00:29:31.360
Please.

408
00:29:31.361 --> 00:29:32.200
We'll take it.

409
00:29:32.201 --> 00:29:33.020
We borrow.

410
00:29:33.021 --> 00:29:34.861
And we're leaving.

411
00:29:38.540 --> 00:29:42.859
Ask him why they left the village
instead of welcoming us.

412
00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:50.280
He says they thought
we were the Sea Peoples.

413
00:29:50.281 --> 00:29:54.380
No. we're Greeks from Ithaca.

414
00:29:54.821 --> 00:29:56.915
You may have heard
about our victory at Troy.

415
00:29:57.561 --> 00:29:59.734
With the southerly winds. we're sailing west.

416
00:30:06.460 --> 00:30:10.160
He says that if you go that way.
you won't like what you find.

417
00:30:11.500 --> 00:30:12.840
After years of war

418
00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:17.500
No one will stop my men
on their way home.

419
00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:20.000
Neither do I.

420
00:30:21.580 --> 00:30:22.880
Nothing happened?

421
00:30:39.160 --> 00:30:40.720
We're chasing the setting sun.

422
00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:47.060
You found him.

423
00:30:50.181 --> 00:30:51.280
You lucky bastard.

424
00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:05.206
No. no. no. no. no.

425
00:31:05.861 --> 00:31:07.760
The sheep are not alone.

426
00:31:09.560 --> 00:31:10.560
Let's go after them.

427
00:32:31.188 --> 00:32:32.188
Someone must live here.

428
00:32:33.810 --> 00:32:35.126
Yes. they make cheese.

429
00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:54.640
It's delicious.

430
00:32:58.019 --> 00:32:59.180
And there's plenty of it.

431
00:32:59.204 --> 00:33:00.920
We have to leave.

432
00:33:01.004 --> 00:33:02.649
We didn't bring anything to trade.

433
00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:04.420
It is Dia's law.

434
00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:08.260
I don't know if the owner is familiar with
the Law of Dia. or even knows who Dia is.

435
00:33:08.344 --> 00:33:10.384
Everyone knows the Law of Dia.

436
00:33:10.617 --> 00:33:11.777
Build a fire.

437
00:33:11.861 --> 00:33:13.735
We'll wait until the owner comes back.

438
00:34:32.854 --> 00:34:34.522
Away from the fire.

439
00:34:34.739 --> 00:34:36.980
No. no!

440
00:34:38.960 --> 00:34:40.120
Take a break!

441
00:36:13.194 --> 00:36:14.828
I think he's asleep.

442
00:36:59.899 --> 00:37:01.201
Wait a minute.

443
00:37:04.143 --> 00:37:05.143
Don't look.

444
00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:14.480
We can't move that rock.

445
00:37:14.481 --> 00:37:15.481
He'll lock us up here.

446
00:37:24.120 --> 00:37:26.600
Tell everyone that this morning.

447
00:37:26.601 --> 00:37:29.864
As soon as he herds the flock outside.
we'll head for the entrance.

448
00:37:30.301 --> 00:37:31.301
Stay close to the wall.

449
00:37:56.140 --> 00:38:01.185
No. no. no. no. no. no!

450
00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:09.640
Run!

451
00:38:17.920 --> 00:38:20.760
Is there anything we can use to block the entrance to the cave?

452
00:38:20.761 --> 00:38:21.761
Leave only the opening.

453
00:38:22.680 --> 00:38:23.680
Maybe.

454
00:38:24.630 --> 00:38:25.630
But how?

455
00:38:26.124 --> 00:38:27.124
It's closed.

456
00:38:30.900 --> 00:38:31.900
He'll notice that.

457
00:38:33.829 --> 00:38:36.320
Grab the thin end
and put it in the fire.

458
00:38:37.400 --> 00:38:38.720
How many ropes do we have left?

459
00:38:39.620 --> 00:38:40.600
How big is that herd?

460
00:38:40.601 --> 00:38:41.700
About twenty. maybe.

461
00:38:44.520 --> 00:38:46.280
There are too many of us.

462
00:38:46.281 --> 00:38:48.840
When he comes back.
two of us have to run outside.

463
00:38:48.841 --> 00:38:50.120
To let oneself be killed.

464
00:38:55.349 --> 00:38:58.760
Run low through the herd toward the exit
and then split up.

465
00:38:58.761 --> 00:39:00.520
That way. he'll catch only one.

466
00:39:00.521 --> 00:39:01.520
That's right.

467
00:39:01.521 --> 00:39:03.240
Four of us have to give it a try.

468
00:39:03.241 --> 00:39:04.080
Two and two.

469
00:39:04.081 --> 00:39:05.640
Why risk more men?

470
00:39:05.641 --> 00:39:07.100
Last night. there were two fewer of us.

471
00:39:07.101 --> 00:39:09.240
We'll let the others know
before they fall asleep.

472
00:39:33.484 --> 00:39:34.956
Run! Run! Run!

473
00:41:13.232 --> 00:41:14.712
And what about his body?

474
00:41:15.160 --> 00:41:16.039
What happened?

475
00:41:16.161 --> 00:41:17.720
Are we just going to let him rot here?

476
00:41:18.794 --> 00:41:20.120
Leave him as food
for that monster?

477
00:41:22.160 --> 00:41:24.960
We honor our dead. Odysseus.

478
00:41:24.961 --> 00:41:26.320
He died to help us escape.

479
00:41:26.321 --> 00:41:27.720
And we'll honor him by running away.

480
00:41:31.840 --> 00:41:34.802
Father Poseidon. they've blinded me.

481
00:41:35.300 --> 00:41:38.553
Avenge me. Father. Avenge my pain.

482
00:41:39.567 --> 00:41:42.092
Poseidon. avenge my pain.

483
00:41:42.292 --> 00:41:43.260
He says.

484
00:41:44.840 --> 00:41:46.160
Why didn't he say anything before?

485
00:41:47.920 --> 00:41:49.200
Do you talk to ants?

486
00:41:50.920 --> 00:41:52.280
Should we try to work something out?

487
00:41:54.874 --> 00:41:57.300
I think we've already gotten past that phase.

488
00:43:07.840 --> 00:43:09.940
What are you doing?

489
00:43:09.941 --> 00:43:10.941
No!

490
00:43:28.580 --> 00:43:31.240
Let's go. Run. run!

491
00:43:31.324 --> 00:43:32.574
Let's go!

492
00:44:00.525 --> 00:44:02.020
Hop on!

493
00:44:03.516 --> 00:44:04.813
Get moving!

494
00:44:25.960 --> 00:44:30.600
Come on. come on!

495
00:44:30.601 --> 00:44:32.445
Let's go!

496
00:44:51.892 --> 00:44:53.692
Why did you shoot that arrow?

497
00:44:53.921 --> 00:44:56.160
Because of that monster. we lost
some brave men.

498
00:44:56.161 --> 00:44:59.178
Yes. and that monster was the son of Poseidon.

499
00:44:59.320 --> 00:45:00.359
What?

500
00:45:00.561 --> 00:45:03.240
When we gouged out his eye. we heard him
call out his father's name.

501
00:45:03.241 --> 00:45:04.880
That thing was a monster.

502
00:45:04.881 --> 00:45:06.020
I heard that. too.

503
00:45:06.849 --> 00:45:08.880
So has the sea become our enemy?

504
00:45:08.881 --> 00:45:09.881
Even the wind.

505
00:45:10.920 --> 00:45:13.440
How will we get home
if Poseidon hates us?

506
00:45:13.441 --> 00:45:15.960
We cannot live under the yoke
of promises and sacrifices.

507
00:45:18.240 --> 00:45:19.920
Do you know how many times you've heard me

508
00:45:19.921 --> 00:45:22.680
to weep and beg Athena—
ever since we left Troy?

509
00:45:22.681 --> 00:45:24.440
Couldn't you have shown a little compassion?

510
00:45:25.559 --> 00:45:26.720
Take this.

511
00:45:28.950 --> 00:45:32.300
Wear this to remember those
whom we could not bury.

512
00:45:34.040 --> 00:45:36.160
The gods help those
who help themselves.

513
00:45:39.920 --> 00:45:42.737
From that moment on. every rough stretch of water
or headwind

514
00:45:42.937 --> 00:45:45.200
He tried to convince the crew
that we were haunted by a curse.

515
00:45:47.684 --> 00:45:48.960
But where's my crew?

516
00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:52.800
When will you let me see my men?

517
00:45:53.840 --> 00:45:56.640
If I could talk to them.
they would help me remember.

518
00:45:56.641 --> 00:45:59.540
Did I have a wife? Children?
Maybe a son?

519
00:45:59.541 --> 00:46:00.840
How long have I been here?

520
00:46:02.640 --> 00:46:03.640
Too long.

521
00:46:04.520 --> 00:46:09.140
If I had a son.
how old would he be now?

522
00:46:11.600 --> 00:46:13.760
I don't know how to talk to the king.

523
00:46:13.761 --> 00:46:15.040
You'll be fine.

524
00:46:15.041 --> 00:46:16.340
Just be quiet.

525
00:46:17.200 --> 00:46:18.520
But if we're asked…

526
00:46:18.521 --> 00:46:20.445
Let's say we're merchants from Crete.

527
00:46:20.645 --> 00:46:22.480
and then we'll see
how we'll approach it.

528
00:46:32.640 --> 00:46:33.920
Welcome.

529
00:46:33.921 --> 00:46:35.120
Thank you.

530
00:46:35.121 --> 00:46:36.160
It looks like you're having fun.

531
00:46:36.161 --> 00:46:37.161
This way.

532
00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:43.640
Where are you taking us?

533
00:46:43.641 --> 00:46:46.160
The king insists
that he meet with the foreigners in person.

534
00:47:07.900 --> 00:47:09.134
Have a seat.

535
00:47:12.312 --> 00:47:13.312
Not you.

536
00:47:15.280 --> 00:47:16.280
You.

537
00:47:18.280 --> 00:47:21.612
Enjoy our hospitality.
just as men should.

538
00:47:22.121 --> 00:47:23.920
But maybe you could tell us who you are.

539
00:47:25.219 --> 00:47:26.939
You came on the right day.

540
00:47:27.081 --> 00:47:29.200
We're celebrating my daughter's wedding.

541
00:47:29.201 --> 00:47:31.540
And tomorrow she's leaving to start a new life.

542
00:47:32.920 --> 00:47:34.320
You don't look like a beggar.

543
00:47:34.681 --> 00:47:36.040
Let's assume you know who I am.

544
00:47:37.960 --> 00:47:42.640
Menelaus. son of Atreus.
and brother of Agamemnon.

545
00:47:43.880 --> 00:47:45.080
And Helena's husband.

546
00:47:46.120 --> 00:47:47.620
He must have heard about Helena.

547
00:47:49.971 --> 00:47:50.971
That face…

548
00:47:51.675 --> 00:47:53.140
She sent out a thousand ships.

549
00:47:55.465 --> 00:47:57.346
And maybe there are only five hundred of them left now.

550
00:48:02.530 --> 00:48:05.460
All those who fell on the plains of Troy
for that face.

551
00:48:05.461 --> 00:48:07.530
Because of your brother's ambition.

552
00:48:07.985 --> 00:48:09.920
That sounds familiar to me.

553
00:48:11.406 --> 00:48:12.406
Aias.

554
00:48:13.499 --> 00:48:14.674
Patroclus…

555
00:48:14.758 --> 00:48:16.357
Achilles…

556
00:48:16.441 --> 00:48:19.906
I don't miss them as much as I miss a single warrior.

557
00:48:20.630 --> 00:48:22.240
He was the smartest of us all.

558
00:48:24.880 --> 00:48:27.040
We languished on that coast for ten years.

559
00:48:38.939 --> 00:48:41.619
No progress on either side.

560
00:48:41.899 --> 00:48:43.978
We used to argue among ourselves.
until one day…

561
00:48:46.880 --> 00:48:48.320
He found a solution.

562
00:48:50.320 --> 00:48:51.320
Odysseus.

563
00:48:52.366 --> 00:48:54.166
Have you heard the story about the horse?

564
00:48:57.606 --> 00:48:59.406
Or do you want to hear it
from someone who was inside?

565
00:49:03.590 --> 00:49:05.040
We stayed there for days on end.

566
00:49:06.390 --> 00:49:09.000
They were crammed into the horse-drawn carriage
before it was pulled into town.

567
00:49:10.366 --> 00:49:12.886
Odysseus knew
that no one must know about us.

568
00:49:12.970 --> 00:49:15.449
not even that soldier.
whom we left behind there.

569
00:49:21.206 --> 00:49:24.355
During the first two high tides.
we almost drowned.

570
00:49:29.494 --> 00:49:30.982
We remained silent.

571
00:49:31.561 --> 00:49:32.760
We were suffocating in our own feces.

572
00:50:00.653 --> 00:50:03.929
A sacrifice to Athena.

573
00:50:06.279 --> 00:50:09.359
Because the gods honor those
who make offerings to them.

574
00:51:31.234 --> 00:51:33.328
We have to burn it.

575
00:51:33.411 --> 00:51:35.768
Burn him! Burn him!

576
00:51:57.586 --> 00:52:00.106
It is an offering to Athena.

577
00:52:07.873 --> 00:52:10.273
It is an offering to Athena.

578
00:54:46.599 --> 00:54:48.599
Goal!

579
00:54:49.037 --> 00:54:52.853
And he knew when. that night.
to slip out from behind the walls of Troy.

580
00:54:59.577 --> 00:55:01.560
Let's go! Let's go!

581
00:55:06.601 --> 00:55:08.120
To the gates!

582
00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:10.440
To the gates!

583
00:55:29.012 --> 00:55:30.828
Let's go! Let's go!

584
00:55:32.160 --> 00:55:33.600
To the gates!

585
00:56:13.466 --> 00:56:14.832
Run!

586
00:56:21.347 --> 00:56:22.837
Hurry!

587
00:56:44.787 --> 00:56:47.573
Come on. hurry up!

588
00:57:19.986 --> 00:57:21.626
I think you know the rest of the story.

589
00:57:21.710 --> 00:57:23.149
Did my story move you?

590
00:57:23.233 --> 00:57:25.152
Does my story move you?
Then you should hear the poets.

591
00:57:25.236 --> 00:57:26.236
I've seen a lot.

592
00:57:29.540 --> 00:57:31.506
But I wasn't with you.

593
00:57:31.590 --> 00:57:34.069
Your father… Odysseus.

594
00:57:37.361 --> 00:57:38.452
Do you still remember him?

595
00:57:39.446 --> 00:57:40.976
When was the last time you saw him?

596
00:57:41.307 --> 00:57:42.307
In Troy.

597
00:57:43.390 --> 00:57:45.480
The gods granted us a well-deserved victory.

598
00:57:45.481 --> 00:57:46.481
Is that fair?

599
00:57:47.469 --> 00:57:51.480
The gods granted us a just victory.
but they did not bless our journey home.

600
00:57:52.509 --> 00:57:55.516
My return took years;
his never happened.

601
00:57:55.747 --> 00:57:57.762
Others returned
and wished it hadn't happened.

602
00:57:58.435 --> 00:57:59.353
Tell him.

603
00:57:59.698 --> 00:58:01.497
Tell him about your brother.

604
00:58:01.581 --> 00:58:02.420
No.

605
00:58:02.421 --> 00:58:05.140
Tell him what fate the gods
have in store for him after his glorious victory.

606
00:58:05.141 --> 00:58:05.980
No.

607
00:58:05.981 --> 00:58:06.980
Tell him how they welcomed him as a hero.

608
00:58:06.981 --> 00:58:07.820
No!

609
00:58:07.821 --> 00:58:10.420
Tell him how Agamemnon
returned home years before us.

610
00:58:10.421 --> 00:58:14.380
Tell him how his wife.
my twin. Clytemnestra. welcomed him.

611
00:58:14.381 --> 00:58:15.420
No… no… no.

612
00:58:15.421 --> 00:58:17.100
Tell him how… No!

613
00:58:17.184 --> 00:58:19.012
He doesn't want to hear about my brother!

614
00:58:21.940 --> 00:58:23.374
And what about his father?

615
00:58:24.021 --> 00:58:26.540
I don't know anything about Odysseus from Troy.

616
00:58:26.541 --> 00:58:27.541
Nothing?

617
00:58:29.055 --> 00:58:30.625
Not even from any of your men?

618
00:58:31.305 --> 00:58:34.060
Not even from Agamemnon’s men.
traitors. or travelers?

619
00:58:34.061 --> 00:58:35.020
Did you come here because of the rumors?

620
00:58:35.021 --> 00:58:36.220
Is that why you came?

621
00:58:36.221 --> 00:58:37.406
Because of the gossip?

622
00:58:37.781 --> 00:58:39.300
Then you've come to the right place.

623
00:58:39.301 --> 00:58:42.580
Do any of you have
a story about Odysseus?

624
00:58:42.767 --> 00:58:45.446
Some say he's rich.
others say he's poor.

625
00:58:46.450 --> 00:58:49.020
Some say he died.
others say he was captured.

626
00:58:49.021 --> 00:58:49.900
What do you think?

627
00:58:49.901 --> 00:58:50.901
Captured?

628
00:58:54.073 --> 00:58:57.513
What prison could hold a man like that?

629
00:58:59.177 --> 00:59:01.140
Tell me what you remember.

630
00:59:01.334 --> 00:59:02.334
And the rest will follow.

631
00:59:03.699 --> 00:59:07.139
And the men began to believe
that I had angered Poseidon.

632
00:59:28.902 --> 00:59:30.902
Ready!

633
00:59:31.260 --> 00:59:36.015
Row!

634
00:59:37.765 --> 00:59:39.582
Row!

635
00:59:53.260 --> 00:59:55.340
Storms came one after another

636
00:59:55.341 --> 00:59:58.940
and led us off course.
until we lost all sense of direction.

637
01:00:51.682 --> 01:00:52.531
Hey!

638
01:00:54.848 --> 01:00:55.993
We came in peace.

639
01:00:58.902 --> 01:01:00.193
We just need a little food.

640
01:01:06.265 --> 01:01:07.759
We need food.

641
01:01:08.995 --> 01:01:10.060
Do you understand?

642
01:01:10.061 --> 01:01:11.061
He's just a child.

643
01:01:14.296 --> 01:01:16.546
If this is a child.
then what are his or her parents like?

644
01:01:19.341 --> 01:01:20.792
Calm down… calm down. everything's fine.

645
01:01:21.221 --> 01:01:22.060
No. no. no. no.

646
01:01:22.061 --> 01:01:23.940
Calm… calm.

647
01:01:42.381 --> 01:01:44.540
Back to the ship!

648
01:02:11.232 --> 01:02:13.178
Come on. back! Back! Let's go!

649
01:02:13.262 --> 01:02:15.008
Damn!

650
01:03:23.000 --> 01:03:24.713
Run! Faster!

651
01:03:34.340 --> 01:03:36.340
To the ship! To the ship!

652
01:03:40.983 --> 01:03:41.708
Let's go!

653
01:03:51.790 --> 01:03:53.790
Let's go! Let's go!

654
01:03:53.874 --> 01:03:55.412
Row!

655
01:03:55.495 --> 01:03:56.194
Wait!

656
01:03:58.173 --> 01:03:59.536
Wait!

657
01:04:00.300 --> 01:04:02.001
Row!

658
01:04:02.085 --> 01:04:04.436
Row!

659
01:04:07.340 --> 01:04:08.340
Run!

660
01:04:09.140 --> 01:04:10.140
Wait!

661
01:04:12.573 --> 01:04:13.893
Wait!

662
01:04:32.206 --> 01:04:34.406
Run. for heaven's sake!

663
01:05:27.780 --> 01:05:30.383
Turn left and follow the coastline.

664
01:05:31.261 --> 01:05:32.300
What if they have ships?

665
01:05:32.301 --> 01:05:34.801
We have to get as far as possible.

666
01:05:39.060 --> 01:05:42.060
We don't have enough supplies to sail
across the sea with no land in sight.

667
01:05:42.061 --> 01:05:43.794
We're lost.

668
01:05:43.878 --> 01:05:47.274
We need to find some food
and figure out where we are.

669
01:05:49.900 --> 01:05:51.140
Turn left.

670
01:06:17.313 --> 01:06:17.950
There!

671
01:06:18.781 --> 01:06:19.860
Smoke… over there!

672
01:06:20.492 --> 01:06:23.224
Go around it; we'll anchor off the coast.

673
01:06:30.666 --> 01:06:31.666
Warning!

674
01:06:35.866 --> 01:06:37.377
Assemble the landing party.

675
01:06:39.660 --> 01:06:40.660
Tomorrow.

676
01:06:43.220 --> 01:06:44.220
Tomorrow.

677
01:06:54.875 --> 01:06:56.568
Have someone stay with the boats.

678
01:06:57.621 --> 01:06:59.257
We agreed that you should stay.

679
01:07:01.385 --> 01:07:02.300
Did you agree?

680
01:07:02.301 --> 01:07:03.320
Everyone.

681
01:07:03.901 --> 01:07:04.901
As long as we live.

682
01:07:16.020 --> 01:07:17.540
Is this a rebellion?

683
01:07:17.541 --> 01:07:19.140
That's up to you.

684
01:07:23.340 --> 01:07:24.340
Give me your bow.

685
01:07:24.341 --> 01:07:25.842
I'll make sure
we have something to eat tonight.

686
01:07:26.461 --> 01:07:27.944
Whatever you find there.

687
01:09:14.861 --> 01:09:15.700
No. no. no. okay!

688
01:09:15.701 --> 01:09:16.970
All right!

689
01:09:20.490 --> 01:09:22.579
You're safe!

690
01:09:22.580 --> 01:09:23.419
Everything's fine!

691
01:09:23.420 --> 01:09:24.579
No one will hurt you!

692
01:09:25.981 --> 01:09:28.499
No one will hurt you.

693
01:09:32.110 --> 01:09:34.060
My name is Eurylochus.
what's your name?

694
01:09:34.110 --> 01:09:35.180
Circe.

695
01:09:35.473 --> 01:09:38.521
Circe. we really need some food.

696
01:09:39.379 --> 01:09:40.379
I have some food.

697
01:09:42.396 --> 01:09:43.526
A lot of food.

698
01:10:05.122 --> 01:10:06.067
May I?

699
01:10:09.220 --> 01:10:10.220
Put down your weapons.

700
01:10:10.221 --> 01:10:11.221
Even the shields.

701
01:10:12.610 --> 01:10:14.340
Leave them outside the door.

702
01:10:56.980 --> 01:10:58.572
Did you like my meat?

703
01:10:59.181 --> 01:11:01.340
Why do you have all of this?

704
01:11:02.660 --> 01:11:05.446
No one knows when the strangers will arrive.

705
01:11:15.481 --> 01:11:17.087
Men.

706
01:11:18.860 --> 01:11:20.620
Their appetite is insatiable.

707
01:11:22.180 --> 01:11:24.260
They can never get enough of them.

708
01:12:40.326 --> 01:12:41.552
Eurylochus.

709
01:13:07.140 --> 01:13:08.220
They haven't come back yet.

710
01:13:08.221 --> 01:13:09.060
Not a single one.

711
01:13:09.061 --> 01:13:09.900
No.

712
01:13:09.901 --> 01:13:10.895
Give me your knife.

713
01:13:14.553 --> 01:13:15.667
Stay by the boat.

714
01:13:56.740 --> 01:13:58.424
Why don't you come in?

715
01:14:06.946 --> 01:14:08.473
Are you coming from the sea?

716
01:14:10.135 --> 01:14:11.420
Are you alone?

717
01:14:16.619 --> 01:14:19.394
It seems my pigs have taken a liking to you.

718
01:14:20.261 --> 01:14:21.500
Are you hungry?

719
01:14:23.780 --> 01:14:25.460
Leave your weapon by the door.

720
01:14:25.461 --> 01:14:27.980
And enjoy Dio's hospitality.

721
01:14:29.340 --> 01:14:30.180
Catch that bastard.

722
01:14:30.181 --> 01:14:32.308
You can serve it for dinner.

723
01:14:57.060 --> 01:15:00.020
Whoever you are.
you're definitely not a farmer.

724
01:15:00.021 --> 01:15:01.021
You're welcome.

725
01:15:04.622 --> 01:15:06.773
That goulash looks delicious.

726
01:15:09.661 --> 01:15:11.530
There's just enough for us.

727
01:15:12.461 --> 01:15:14.460
Who said we're alone?

728
01:15:15.930 --> 01:15:17.460
Who else is here?

729
01:15:17.461 --> 01:15:18.700
That's a good question.

730
01:15:32.020 --> 01:15:35.926
You say you're alone.
but no one comes here alone.

731
01:15:36.781 --> 01:15:37.719
That's right.

732
01:15:38.301 --> 01:15:41.180
Because there is no road leading here.
only the sea.

733
01:15:41.181 --> 01:15:43.630
So where are your companions. then?

734
01:15:44.341 --> 01:15:45.892
I'm looking for them.

735
01:15:46.741 --> 01:15:48.509
Has anyone been through here?

736
01:15:51.425 --> 01:15:53.140
But you prepared all this food.

737
01:15:53.141 --> 01:15:54.660
Are you hearing my confession?

738
01:15:54.661 --> 01:15:55.661
You. me?

739
01:15:57.545 --> 01:16:02.300
A starving pilgrim
who won't touch the food in front of him.

740
01:16:02.301 --> 01:16:05.826
And he claims to be a sailor.
but he wears the armor of a great warrior.

741
01:16:06.781 --> 01:16:12.180
And he breaks Dia's law by bringing
a knife into his hostess's house.

742
01:16:12.181 --> 01:16:13.940
You have keen eyes.

743
01:16:13.941 --> 01:16:15.612
The gods filed them down for me.

744
01:16:16.181 --> 01:16:17.672
And do they give you foresight?

745
01:16:18.341 --> 01:16:19.341
More like the truth.

746
01:16:20.625 --> 01:16:22.300
Sometimes.

747
01:16:22.301 --> 01:16:24.749
And they'll tell you.
"Where can I find my men?"

748
01:16:25.261 --> 01:16:26.420
Your men?

749
01:16:26.421 --> 01:16:28.180
Yes.

750
01:16:28.181 --> 01:16:29.300
You'll tell me.

751
01:16:29.301 --> 01:16:31.340
So. are you going to tell me?

752
01:16:31.341 --> 01:16:32.341
Maybe.

753
01:16:34.154 --> 01:16:36.059
If you give me your knife.

754
01:16:44.460 --> 01:16:48.620
Why would I help a liar
who refuses my hospitality?

755
01:16:58.620 --> 01:17:00.300
Your men were here just a moment ago.

756
01:17:00.301 --> 01:17:01.820
No… they aren't.

757
01:17:08.140 --> 01:17:09.740
Did they leave?

758
01:17:09.741 --> 01:17:10.741
Yes.

759
01:17:13.580 --> 01:17:15.420
And did they leave their rings here?

760
01:17:23.260 --> 01:17:24.340
I wouldn't do that.

761
01:17:25.940 --> 01:17:26.780
Don't do that.

762
01:17:26.781 --> 01:17:28.660
I don't know who this stranger is to you.

763
01:17:28.661 --> 01:17:32.020
But if you don't drop the knife.
I'll keep choking her until I find out.

764
01:17:32.021 --> 01:17:33.140
Don't touch her.

765
01:17:33.141 --> 01:17:34.300
Is she a relative of yours?

766
01:17:36.340 --> 01:17:37.340
Your mother?

767
01:17:39.979 --> 01:17:40.979
Your sister?

768
01:17:44.580 --> 01:17:46.201
Did you do the same thing to her
as you did to my husband?

769
01:17:46.401 --> 01:17:47.380
Did you turn them into pigs?

770
01:17:47.381 --> 01:17:49.660
I didn't turn
your men into anything.

771
01:17:49.661 --> 01:17:50.580
Take a look at them.

772
01:17:50.581 --> 01:17:52.940
That's what they really look like…
Take a look at them.

773
01:17:53.024 --> 01:17:57.183
Their filthy. animalistic instincts…
the very ones you glorify in war.

774
01:17:57.920 --> 01:18:00.880
Because of you. they raped and plundered the world.

775
01:18:01.021 --> 01:18:02.580
And don't you think that's what they're all about?

776
01:18:02.581 --> 01:18:05.300
Do you think they did all that
just because you told them to?

777
01:18:05.301 --> 01:18:09.740
A true leader convinces himself that the brutality
of his men is merely discipline.

778
01:18:09.741 --> 01:18:11.860
But you don't seem naive.

779
01:18:11.861 --> 01:18:15.380
And yet You sent them to me
when I was alone.

780
01:18:15.381 --> 01:18:19.157
A handful of dirty soldiers.
hungry. with boiling blood in their veins.

781
01:18:21.220 --> 01:18:24.080
Did he think they would respect
me and my home?

782
01:18:24.781 --> 01:18:27.845
Take a look at them and discover
the truth about your men.

783
01:18:28.980 --> 01:18:30.302
Did someone hurt you?

784
01:18:30.386 --> 01:18:32.305
They would hurt someone.

785
01:18:32.541 --> 01:18:34.700
They just want to go home.

786
01:18:37.740 --> 01:18:38.740
Let them come back.

787
01:19:02.959 --> 01:19:05.719
Come back. come back. come back.

788
01:19:10.785 --> 01:19:12.940
Return to your true form.

789
01:19:16.868 --> 01:19:20.260
Return.
return to your true form.

790
01:19:21.280 --> 01:19:24.180
Return.
return to your true form.

791
01:19:24.890 --> 01:19:27.620
Come back. come back. come back.

792
01:19:30.579 --> 01:19:33.557
Return to your true form.

793
01:19:38.045 --> 01:19:41.100
Odysseus. let's go to the ship.

794
01:19:41.101 --> 01:19:44.577
Take as much food as you can carry.
and leave the meat alone.

795
01:19:44.941 --> 01:19:47.660
You won't let her live—she's a witch.

796
01:19:47.661 --> 01:19:49.220
Who gave her this power?

797
01:19:49.221 --> 01:19:50.580
The Gods.

798
01:19:50.581 --> 01:19:52.420
Fill the jugs and containers from the stream.

799
01:19:52.421 --> 01:19:53.928
At dawn. get ready to set sail.

800
01:19:59.720 --> 01:20:01.192
Why are you letting her do that?

801
01:20:01.778 --> 01:20:03.483
We're better off this way.

802
01:20:05.021 --> 01:20:06.980
I apologize if we offended you.

803
01:20:08.181 --> 01:20:09.993
We won't leave without your help.

804
01:20:10.541 --> 01:20:12.380
We need a route
to the trade routes.

805
01:20:12.381 --> 01:20:14.180
You've wandered around long enough.

806
01:20:14.181 --> 01:20:15.193
What do you mean?

807
01:20:16.780 --> 01:20:19.660
No one but the seer Teiresias
will show you the way.

808
01:20:19.661 --> 01:20:20.820
Teiresias?

809
01:20:20.821 --> 01:20:22.420
In Hád.

810
01:20:22.421 --> 01:20:25.900
No one can go to Hades
and come back.

811
01:20:25.901 --> 01:20:26.901
But you'll come back.

812
01:20:28.060 --> 01:20:30.460
Head north to the Land of Twilight.

813
01:20:35.936 --> 01:20:40.246
Then walk along the land until the river of fire
meets the river of ice.

814
01:21:31.211 --> 01:21:33.139
Dig a trench there.

815
01:21:35.745 --> 01:21:38.232
And fill it with the blood of the sacrifice.

816
01:21:58.180 --> 01:21:59.700
And gather the souls of the dead.

817
01:22:00.605 --> 01:22:02.940
Then you'll find out how to get home.

818
01:22:04.560 --> 01:22:05.560
You.

819
01:22:06.860 --> 01:22:07.940
Your men aren't.

820
01:22:08.815 --> 01:22:10.660
Do you curse them?

821
01:22:10.661 --> 01:22:11.820
That's not a curse.

822
01:22:13.062 --> 01:22:14.062
That's true.

823
01:22:15.223 --> 01:22:17.852
Your men don't want
you to provoke the gods.

824
01:22:19.619 --> 01:22:22.109
Because the gods say
that only you will return home.

825
01:22:22.941 --> 01:22:24.860
Then I'll provoke the gods.

826
01:23:05.392 --> 01:23:06.612
Sinon.

827
01:23:07.093 --> 01:23:08.253
I am Odysseus.

828
01:23:13.306 --> 01:23:14.306
Sinon.

829
01:23:15.533 --> 01:23:16.693
I am Odysseus.

830
01:23:19.080 --> 01:23:20.781
Let him have a drink. Odysseus.

831
01:23:21.601 --> 01:23:23.531
And then you'll hear his testimony.

832
01:23:37.983 --> 01:23:38.735
Odysseus.

833
01:23:39.284 --> 01:23:40.440
Yes.

834
01:23:40.441 --> 01:23:42.360
You lied to everyone.

835
01:23:46.279 --> 01:23:47.822
Or were you supposed to lie to me?

836
01:23:49.386 --> 01:23:50.998
I had to convince you.

837
01:23:52.615 --> 01:23:54.613
Why did you sign up voluntarily?

838
01:23:54.761 --> 01:23:56.520
Do you remember how I got here?

839
01:23:56.521 --> 01:23:59.800
I asked if I could
take Antino's place.

840
01:23:59.801 --> 01:24:01.600
He was selected by lottery.

841
01:24:01.601 --> 01:24:04.985
And he offered your family money
if you took his place.

842
01:24:05.987 --> 01:24:08.100
Unless the Trojans let me live.

843
01:24:10.098 --> 01:24:11.516
And if I don't come home.

844
01:24:12.308 --> 01:24:14.052
Give this to my father.

845
01:24:15.408 --> 01:24:16.942
And tell him what I did.

846
01:24:20.050 --> 01:24:21.050
Odysseus.

847
01:24:23.100 --> 01:24:25.420
And don't hesitate to add a few details.

848
01:24:28.640 --> 01:24:30.600
You didn't trust me enough
to tell me the truth.

849
01:24:32.706 --> 01:24:34.506
You knew I would die for you.

850
01:24:34.590 --> 01:24:36.389
I had to convince you.

851
01:24:36.473 --> 01:24:39.952
I died because of your lie
on that shore.

852
01:24:40.036 --> 01:24:41.016
I know.

853
01:24:41.241 --> 01:24:42.920
I was there with you.

854
01:24:43.004 --> 01:24:47.600
And I heard you tell that lie
as you were dying. and we won the war.

855
01:24:47.601 --> 01:24:50.280
I was just a few steps away from you.
I could smell your blood.

856
01:24:50.281 --> 01:24:53.393
And what comfort is that supposed to give me?

857
01:24:54.860 --> 01:24:58.440
I didn't know you were in on it
until the dead told me.

858
01:24:59.676 --> 01:25:00.639
Which dead people?

859
01:25:01.242 --> 01:25:04.320
There are a lot of your kind wandering around here.

860
01:25:06.931 --> 01:25:09.635
The dead are gathering
to mourn the living

861
01:25:09.835 --> 01:25:11.659
and welcomed the newcomers.

862
01:25:12.161 --> 01:25:15.080
And my father was the first to come to me.

863
01:25:15.081 --> 01:25:16.560
To comfort you?

864
01:25:16.834 --> 01:25:18.181
Souls do not bring comfort.

865
01:25:19.098 --> 01:25:22.380
They are looking for those who have been wronged by the living.

866
01:25:22.975 --> 01:25:26.960
My father died poor and alone.
begging for crumbs

867
01:25:27.176 --> 01:25:30.520
from people like Antinous.

868
01:25:30.604 --> 01:25:32.389
Did Antinous cheat on you?

869
01:25:32.881 --> 01:25:35.280
And he's definitely cheating on you. too.

870
01:25:35.281 --> 01:25:36.120
How?

871
01:25:36.121 --> 01:25:38.960
Who is taking care of your wife
and son in Ithaca?

872
01:25:38.961 --> 01:25:41.200
I'm not worried about my wife.

873
01:25:42.200 --> 01:25:45.400
Talk to Agamemnon about returning home.

874
01:25:45.401 --> 01:25:47.000
Is Agamemnon here?

875
01:25:50.338 --> 01:25:53.658
You buried me with honor in Troy;
thank you.

876
01:25:54.525 --> 01:25:56.760
But think also of those
whom you have not buried.

877
01:25:57.167 --> 01:25:59.240
That's not always possible.

878
01:25:59.600 --> 01:26:01.043
When you get home.

879
01:26:01.894 --> 01:26:05.760
Take a boat and sail westward. into the unknown.
until you reach land.

880
01:26:05.761 --> 01:26:09.520
And there. offer sacrifices
and honor your men.

881
01:26:12.093 --> 01:26:13.536
Do you still have that lottery ticket?

882
01:26:17.313 --> 01:26:18.330
Of course.

883
01:26:18.601 --> 01:26:22.160
When you get home.
let Antinoo taste humiliation.

884
01:26:22.161 --> 01:26:23.161
I'll do it.

885
01:26:34.480 --> 01:26:35.480
My lord.

886
01:26:47.040 --> 01:26:48.272
Odysseus.

887
01:26:49.041 --> 01:26:50.378
How did you get here?

888
01:26:50.801 --> 01:26:52.935
We won the war.
and you were on your way home.

889
01:26:53.653 --> 01:26:57.500
Odysseus. the most brilliant
of all my commanders.

890
01:26:58.250 --> 01:26:59.960
They wouldn't have caught you in a trap the way they caught me.

891
01:26:59.961 --> 01:27:02.600
Did the waves wash you ashore just like us?

892
01:27:02.914 --> 01:27:05.720
No. my misfortune was
that I returned home.

893
01:27:07.061 --> 01:27:09.071
So that my beautiful wife can welcome me.

894
01:27:15.177 --> 01:27:16.193
She washed me.

895
01:27:18.680 --> 01:27:19.680
And she put on some perfume.

896
01:27:22.358 --> 01:27:23.399
And I calmed down.

897
01:27:24.672 --> 01:27:26.272
Then she taught me the hardest lesson.

898
01:27:27.046 --> 01:27:33.420
I planted poison in her heart
years ago.

899
01:27:33.504 --> 01:27:38.572
No! No!

900
01:27:40.335 --> 01:27:41.520
And a large part of her heart had rotted away…

901
01:27:41.604 --> 01:27:43.469
For years. she conspired with her lover
to kill me.

902
01:27:44.598 --> 01:27:47.413
Odysseus… Yes.

903
01:27:48.169 --> 01:27:50.999
Did my son Orestes… avenge me?

904
01:27:51.504 --> 01:27:53.326
My goodness. how am I supposed to know that?

905
01:27:54.140 --> 01:27:56.280
I'm still trying to get home.

906
01:27:56.726 --> 01:27:58.058
Don't make the same mistake I did.

907
01:27:59.383 --> 01:28:02.513
Don't walk in through the front door. and don't expect
wreaths and praise.

908
01:28:03.223 --> 01:28:04.183
Enter secretly.

909
01:28:05.229 --> 01:28:06.386
Make time for it.

910
01:28:07.366 --> 01:28:08.327
Gather your strength.

911
01:28:15.583 --> 01:28:16.499
What do you see?

912
01:28:18.461 --> 01:28:19.566
I know what I have to do.

913
01:28:20.082 --> 01:28:21.439
You know only part of what you need to do.

914
01:28:21.920 --> 01:28:23.953
My prophecy is not meant for their ears.

915
01:28:25.608 --> 01:28:26.840
Go back to the ship.

916
01:28:28.819 --> 01:28:31.429
Sail southwest.
around Siren Island.

917
01:28:32.957 --> 01:28:34.960
They lure men onto the cliffs.

918
01:28:35.170 --> 01:28:36.249
Everyone will die.

919
01:28:36.333 --> 01:28:37.731
I won't let that happen.

920
01:28:38.376 --> 01:28:40.480
I'll plug their ears with wax.

921
01:28:40.797 --> 01:28:45.983
Beyond the Sirens. you must choose between the whirlpool
of Charybdis and the monster Scylla.

922
01:28:46.290 --> 01:28:48.616
The whirlpool in the sea will swallow you all up.

923
01:28:48.758 --> 01:28:50.879
The monster hiding in the rocks
will kill only six.

924
01:28:51.379 --> 01:28:55.582
But your stubborn refusal to sacrifice even
your soul will kill them all.

925
01:28:56.722 --> 01:28:57.839
I'll do whatever it takes.

926
01:28:57.923 --> 01:29:00.552
And finally. when you're threatened by hunger and rebellion.

927
01:29:00.636 --> 01:29:04.466
They will ask you to land on the island
where the Sun God grazes his cattle.

928
01:29:04.550 --> 01:29:06.683
If they kill Apollo's cattle...

929
01:29:07.768 --> 01:29:09.346
Zeus will destroy them as punishment.

930
01:29:09.909 --> 01:29:10.698
What do you see?

931
01:29:11.922 --> 01:29:12.979
I'll stop them.

932
01:29:13.215 --> 01:29:16.703
Why do you value the lives of these men
more than the ones you've already lost?

933
01:29:16.787 --> 01:29:19.580
I can still save them from the gods.

934
01:29:19.921 --> 01:29:21.645
But not in front of them.

935
01:29:24.240 --> 01:29:25.537
What is it?

936
01:29:25.640 --> 01:29:27.188
These are the members of your crew
who were not buried.

937
01:29:30.365 --> 01:29:32.733
They're calling on you to share in their shame.

938
01:29:34.722 --> 01:29:35.876
Let's go. hurry up!

939
01:29:50.984 --> 01:29:52.897
Untie the ropes!

940
01:29:53.640 --> 01:29:55.857
We have to set sail!

941
01:30:20.756 --> 01:30:22.051
Is that our goal?

942
01:30:24.586 --> 01:30:26.586
Is this our fate?

943
01:30:46.912 --> 01:30:52.470
We left Hades and lost
many species.

944
01:30:53.426 --> 01:30:55.304
I have to save those who are left.

945
01:30:56.073 --> 01:30:57.332
What happened to them?

946
01:30:57.960 --> 01:30:59.719
Did they leave me here?

947
01:30:59.803 --> 01:31:01.562
Will they come back?

948
01:31:03.322 --> 01:31:08.038
Did you do something to them?

949
01:31:08.122 --> 01:31:09.085
Go ahead.

950
01:31:09.601 --> 01:31:11.960
You told me
not to eat the lotus flower.

951
01:31:12.044 --> 01:31:15.225
Just for tonight. so you can get some sleep.

952
01:31:18.955 --> 01:31:22.073
And tomorrow. when you're ready.
you'll remember.

953
01:31:25.355 --> 01:31:28.652
You'll think of your crew
and your family.

954
01:31:28.736 --> 01:31:29.736
Family?

955
01:31:32.135 --> 01:31:33.135
A woman?

956
01:31:34.995 --> 01:31:36.520
Do I have a wife?

957
01:31:40.840 --> 01:31:41.920
What are you doing?

958
01:31:42.070 --> 01:31:43.920
It's okay. Melanthó.

959
01:31:44.070 --> 01:31:45.480
Foreigners are not allowed here.

960
01:31:45.680 --> 01:31:50.080
Whether there's a curtain or not. after three years
of feasting. we're no longer strangers here.

961
01:31:50.230 --> 01:31:53.520
Do you want to marry me.
or ruin my reputation?

962
01:31:53.604 --> 01:31:55.174
Melanthó knows how to keep a secret.

963
01:31:55.258 --> 01:31:58.658
Actually. I came
because of your reputation.

964
01:31:58.852 --> 01:31:59.852
Really?

965
01:32:00.110 --> 01:32:01.480
Everyone can see how hard you're trying.

966
01:32:02.938 --> 01:32:04.538
Slowly but surely.

967
01:32:05.600 --> 01:32:07.789
But every night you unravel
what you wove during the day.

968
01:32:13.624 --> 01:32:14.881
Leave us alone.

969
01:32:16.635 --> 01:32:18.561
You can't expect her to.

970
01:32:18.645 --> 01:32:20.351
that she would be faithful to the man
she had never met.

971
01:32:20.435 --> 01:32:22.354
I expect her to be faithful to me.

972
01:32:22.438 --> 01:32:24.679
And it will be. when you marry me.

973
01:32:25.833 --> 01:32:29.512
Children have no other choice
but to remain faithful to the future.

974
01:32:29.596 --> 01:32:32.035
And this house is waiting for its master.

975
01:32:32.119 --> 01:32:34.496
just as Ithaca awaits its king.

976
01:32:36.928 --> 01:32:38.726
The King of Ithaca will return.

977
01:32:39.014 --> 01:32:40.253
No. he won't.

978
01:32:42.050 --> 01:32:45.035
Did you know that when I was a kid. I tried
to go with him to Troy?

979
01:32:45.847 --> 01:32:47.292
He never told me that.

980
01:32:48.527 --> 01:32:50.166
Laertes refused to let me go.

981
01:32:50.250 --> 01:32:53.436
He knew that Ithaca needed
a future without him.

982
01:33:00.277 --> 01:33:02.429
And he saw that I am part of
that future.

983
01:33:07.062 --> 01:33:12.185
I won't tell the other suitors
about your deception.

984
01:33:14.693 --> 01:33:15.977
Continue weaving.

985
01:33:18.014 --> 01:33:19.271
It's time to let it go.

986
01:33:28.930 --> 01:33:31.420
Why would you marry someone
who wants someone else?

987
01:33:34.515 --> 01:33:38.800
Your loyalty to the House of Laertus
is your most valuable quality.

988
01:33:42.540 --> 01:33:43.560
I love you.

989
01:33:44.540 --> 01:33:48.040
I want you to choose me
and put an end to this.

990
01:33:49.179 --> 01:33:51.467
Don't say you're not tired.

991
01:33:52.354 --> 01:33:54.679
The waiting… and the uncertainty.

992
01:33:57.234 --> 01:34:04.487
You have fulfilled your duty
year after year.

993
01:34:07.297 --> 01:34:08.845
It's time to think about yourself.

994
01:34:10.743 --> 01:34:11.824
It's time to live again.

995
01:34:17.669 --> 01:34:19.109
Laertes wouldn't want that.

996
01:34:26.400 --> 01:34:27.800
I'll take care of all your needs.

997
01:34:32.421 --> 01:34:33.375
My queen.

998
01:34:36.666 --> 01:34:37.746
And what about my son?

999
01:34:39.440 --> 01:34:40.776
The throne is his.

1000
01:34:41.247 --> 01:34:42.406
He is the heir.

1001
01:34:44.699 --> 01:34:46.539
Unless the two of us have a child.

1002
01:34:56.863 --> 01:34:58.193
Where is Telemachus?

1003
01:35:00.127 --> 01:35:03.707
He went inland
to visit his grandfather.

1004
01:35:11.142 --> 01:35:12.318
He's lying.

1005
01:35:12.767 --> 01:35:14.286
He set sail.

1006
01:35:28.626 --> 01:35:30.333
He went to find out what was going on with his father.

1007
01:35:30.994 --> 01:35:31.908
Where?

1008
01:35:32.260 --> 01:35:33.539
He went to Sparta.

1009
01:35:33.623 --> 01:35:34.623
To Menelaus.

1010
01:35:35.801 --> 01:35:37.113
Okay. shall we go there?

1011
01:35:38.500 --> 01:35:39.500
No.

1012
01:35:40.953 --> 01:35:42.113
Your voice is too loud. Melanthius.

1013
01:35:42.197 --> 01:35:44.488
I told you you're a coward.

1014
01:35:44.728 --> 01:35:45.773
He was listening to us.

1015
01:35:45.857 --> 01:35:47.697
No. no.

1016
01:35:50.115 --> 01:35:51.742
I came to pick up Argos.

1017
01:35:52.879 --> 01:35:54.600
I told you that the dog would stay there.

1018
01:35:54.601 --> 01:35:55.720
Go back to the farm.

1019
01:35:55.721 --> 01:35:58.109
And when we need pigs.
we'll send for you. Melanthius.

1020
01:35:58.261 --> 01:36:00.660
You are no longer welcome in this palace. old man.

1021
01:36:00.744 --> 01:36:03.063
I was following my mistress's orders.

1022
01:36:05.112 --> 01:36:06.438
Do you even know your name?

1023
01:36:07.579 --> 01:36:09.039
Get out.

1024
01:36:09.361 --> 01:36:13.560
Telemachus will have to return
through the temple at Pylos.

1025
01:36:13.561 --> 01:36:15.600
Go there with your men.

1026
01:36:15.601 --> 01:36:17.800
So that you can stay here
and compete for the queen?

1027
01:36:17.801 --> 01:36:18.904
I don't think so.

1028
01:36:19.681 --> 01:36:21.317
He must die on the road.

1029
01:36:21.601 --> 01:36:23.010
And Eurymachus has a plan.

1030
01:36:24.001 --> 01:36:25.001
Eurymachus.

1031
01:36:27.260 --> 01:36:29.160
I want everyone who falls
into the trap to be dead.

1032
01:36:29.161 --> 01:36:31.520
Thrown into a pit with their throats slit.

1033
01:36:31.521 --> 01:36:33.464
Telemachus and his men
will be ready

1034
01:36:33.465 --> 01:36:35.931
against the bandits who are running rampant
on the road between Ithaca and Pylos.

1035
01:36:37.041 --> 01:36:40.465
But what if some of them arrive
at the temple before them?

1036
01:36:41.975 --> 01:36:44.202
Then bring me proof
of Telemachus's death.

1037
01:36:45.561 --> 01:36:46.683
His nose.

1038
01:36:47.201 --> 01:36:49.178
And you'll recognize him just by his nose?

1039
01:36:52.520 --> 01:36:53.840
Even by the ears.

1040
01:36:59.260 --> 01:37:00.172
Who taught you?

1041
01:37:00.406 --> 01:37:02.066
My father's pig herder.

1042
01:37:02.201 --> 01:37:03.600
A pig herder?

1043
01:37:03.675 --> 01:37:05.720
He was the most loyal of his servants.

1044
01:37:05.895 --> 01:37:08.760
Odysseus left behind
his most loyal servants.

1045
01:37:08.910 --> 01:37:10.519
He was always the smartest of them all.

1046
01:37:11.350 --> 01:37:13.667
And the only one Agamemnon listened to.

1047
01:37:14.590 --> 01:37:16.440
I wish I had listened to him more.

1048
01:37:16.441 --> 01:37:17.915
May I ask
what happened to your brother?

1049
01:37:18.260 --> 01:37:20.461
His wife betrayed him and killed him.

1050
01:37:21.390 --> 01:37:22.390
And what happened to her?

1051
01:37:22.550 --> 01:37:24.613
His son Orestes killed them both.

1052
01:37:25.430 --> 01:37:26.812
Did he kill his mother?

1053
01:37:28.001 --> 01:37:29.372
He avenged his father.

1054
01:37:29.971 --> 01:37:31.366
But don't worry.

1055
01:37:31.635 --> 01:37:34.066
Your mother is nothing
like Clytemnestra.

1056
01:37:34.650 --> 01:37:36.825
Maybe not. but the suitors
convinced the elders.

1057
01:37:37.025 --> 01:37:38.680
that it's time for her to get married again.

1058
01:37:38.780 --> 01:37:41.455
Yes. it is not wise
for the throne to remain empty.

1059
01:37:42.012 --> 01:37:44.447
I've heard stories…
about the Sea Peoples.

1060
01:37:44.681 --> 01:37:45.520
Have you been attacked?

1061
01:37:45.521 --> 01:37:48.400
Not yet. but the attacks are coming.

1062
01:37:48.401 --> 01:37:49.720
Sparta is ready.

1063
01:37:49.721 --> 01:37:50.801
And Ithaca?

1064
01:37:51.241 --> 01:37:53.640
He can raise an army
and defend himself.

1065
01:37:53.641 --> 01:37:55.120
But someone has to take the throne.

1066
01:37:55.195 --> 01:37:57.570
Then you must fight there. not here. my son.

1067
01:37:58.146 --> 01:38:00.606
You ran away from the problem.

1068
01:38:00.841 --> 01:38:03.557
And he left his mother
at the mercy of her suitors.

1069
01:38:04.367 --> 01:38:08.155
Should I hire mercenaries and go back
to kill them all?

1070
01:38:08.700 --> 01:38:11.480
Kill the rich and powerful
guests in your own home?

1071
01:38:11.681 --> 01:38:13.760
They will drive you into exile.

1072
01:38:13.761 --> 01:38:14.660
And then what?

1073
01:38:14.961 --> 01:38:17.948
You must drive them out without
breaking the law of Dia.

1074
01:38:19.841 --> 01:38:21.592
I must take the throne.

1075
01:38:22.601 --> 01:38:24.500
And I have to find out
if Odysseus is still alive.

1076
01:38:25.201 --> 01:38:27.812
This has nothing to do with his father.
whom he never knew.

1077
01:38:28.601 --> 01:38:32.409
You must lead. my son.
no matter what fate awaits Odysseus.

1078
01:38:46.160 --> 01:38:47.800
You have to remember.

1079
01:38:47.801 --> 01:38:48.875
No. I don't want to.

1080
01:38:49.721 --> 01:38:50.960
I don't need to know the truth.

1081
01:38:50.961 --> 01:38:52.960
I'm happy here with you.

1082
01:38:54.940 --> 01:38:59.044
Then why are you gathering the wreckage of your
old ship to build a raft?

1083
01:39:01.255 --> 01:39:02.719
Think about your crew…

1084
01:39:04.328 --> 01:39:05.652
and think back on your journey.

1085
01:39:09.455 --> 01:39:11.517
I heard the song "Sirens."

1086
01:39:21.320 --> 01:39:23.500
Plug your ears with wax. guys.

1087
01:39:24.121 --> 01:39:24.960
Not me.

1088
01:39:24.961 --> 01:39:26.200
Tie me to the mast.

1089
01:39:26.201 --> 01:39:27.040
Why?

1090
01:39:27.041 --> 01:39:28.920
I want to hear the song "Sirén."

1091
01:39:28.921 --> 01:39:30.280
No.

1092
01:39:30.281 --> 01:39:31.840
That's a bad idea.

1093
01:39:31.841 --> 01:39:33.400
No one survived
who heard their song.

1094
01:39:33.401 --> 01:39:34.560
That's why it's a good idea.

1095
01:39:34.561 --> 01:39:36.187
I'll be the first to do it.

1096
01:39:36.467 --> 01:39:41.746
No matter how much I beg you.
don't untie me until we've sailed past.

1097
01:39:41.921 --> 01:39:44.000
Take care of the earwax in men's ears.

1098
01:39:44.001 --> 01:39:47.440
A double serving for anyone
who objects!

1099
01:40:45.399 --> 01:40:47.071
What was the song "Sirén" like?

1100
01:40:48.021 --> 01:40:50.210
Everything you've ever wanted.

1101
01:40:50.907 --> 01:40:55.066
And then everything you ever wished for—
things you wish you’d never wished for.

1102
01:41:12.780 --> 01:41:16.107
It was like a delightful itch.
one that makes you want to scratch.

1103
01:41:17.066 --> 01:41:19.558
You'll find it beneath the skin. and the more
you touch it. the more you want it.

1104
01:41:21.986 --> 01:41:24.517
And that amazing feeling becomes unbearable.

1105
01:41:28.900 --> 01:41:30.800
Didn't I tell you that what
you want most.

1106
01:41:33.026 --> 01:41:34.903
Is it something you can't have?

1107
01:41:38.380 --> 01:41:43.009
And what you can't have
is what you once had.

1108
01:41:44.233 --> 01:41:45.495
And you lost it.

1109
01:41:53.766 --> 01:41:56.456
It was a song about all the promises
I hadn't kept.

1110
01:42:01.557 --> 01:42:03.844
And they told me that. in fact.
I don't want to go home.

1111
01:42:07.069 --> 01:42:08.612
Currents!

1112
01:42:09.039 --> 01:42:10.639
Currents ahead!

1113
01:42:12.887 --> 01:42:13.887
Charybdis.

1114
01:42:15.453 --> 01:42:17.293
A wild whirlpool.

1115
01:42:19.789 --> 01:42:21.140
Take down the sails!

1116
01:42:21.141 --> 01:42:22.580
Let's go. hurry up!

1117
01:42:22.581 --> 01:42:24.660
Take the helm!

1118
01:42:24.661 --> 01:42:25.820
When we set sail.

1119
01:42:25.821 --> 01:42:27.740
It's already pulling us in.

1120
01:42:29.635 --> 01:42:30.140
To the ropes!

1121
01:42:30.781 --> 01:42:32.371
We have to go north! Hurry up!

1122
01:42:33.817 --> 01:42:34.937
Maybe we'll make it to that strait.

1123
01:42:35.021 --> 01:42:36.500
It's too narrow.

1124
01:42:36.501 --> 01:42:38.100
Head toward the vortex.

1125
01:42:38.101 --> 01:42:39.629
If we turn the boat sideways toward the slope.

1126
01:42:39.713 --> 01:42:42.024
we'll gain enough speed to
get to the other side.

1127
01:42:42.141 --> 01:42:43.862
Don't worry. we'll manage.

1128
01:42:48.990 --> 01:42:50.520
No. no!

1129
01:42:51.140 --> 01:42:52.313
We're heading toward the canal.

1130
01:42:52.781 --> 01:42:54.580
Quiet! We're heading toward the canal!

1131
01:42:54.581 --> 01:42:55.581
Pull in the oars!

1132
01:42:57.580 --> 01:42:59.340
Oars down!

1133
01:42:59.341 --> 01:43:02.120
Starboard oars. get ready
to stop the boat!

1134
01:43:09.340 --> 01:43:10.580
On my command!

1135
01:43:11.733 --> 01:43:12.733
Three!

1136
01:43:14.573 --> 01:43:15.573
Two!

1137
01:43:16.460 --> 01:43:17.620
One!

1138
01:43:18.054 --> 01:43:19.894
Go for it!

1139
01:43:20.265 --> 01:43:21.302
Pull on the oars!

1140
01:43:32.024 --> 01:43:33.758
Hang in there!

1141
01:44:07.012 --> 01:44:08.099
We did it!

1142
01:44:08.659 --> 01:44:09.665
We did it!

1143
01:44:10.481 --> 01:44:12.474
We did it. The gods are with us.

1144
01:44:16.155 --> 01:44:17.244
Row!

1145
01:44:17.444 --> 01:44:19.354
Row!

1146
01:44:19.471 --> 01:44:21.872
Row!

1147
01:44:21.956 --> 01:44:23.216
Row!

1148
01:44:23.300 --> 01:44:24.740
Keep the boat on an even keel!

1149
01:44:30.376 --> 01:44:32.376
Row!

1150
01:44:32.460 --> 01:44:33.820
Keep the boat steady!

1151
01:44:33.903 --> 01:44:36.924
Go for it!

1152
01:44:39.296 --> 01:44:40.538
Go for it!

1153
01:45:00.169 --> 01:45:01.662
You knew it!

1154
01:45:02.020 --> 01:45:03.377
And you didn't tell us anything!

1155
01:45:03.621 --> 01:45:06.220
If I told you that.
you wouldn't choose this path.

1156
01:45:06.221 --> 01:45:08.540
And those men would be alive!

1157
01:45:08.541 --> 01:45:09.380
No!

1158
01:45:09.381 --> 01:45:10.700
We would all die!

1159
01:45:10.701 --> 01:45:11.980
You had no right to do that!

1160
01:45:11.981 --> 01:45:13.740
It was my full right!

1161
01:45:13.741 --> 01:45:15.500
And I take full responsibility!

1162
01:45:15.501 --> 01:45:18.660
The whirlpool would have destroyed the ship
and drowned all of us!

1163
01:45:18.661 --> 01:45:19.660
That's how we survived!

1164
01:45:19.661 --> 01:45:21.020
Some of us!

1165
01:45:21.021 --> 01:45:22.021
Yes!

1166
01:45:25.620 --> 01:45:27.480
What else did Teiresias tell you?

1167
01:45:39.060 --> 01:45:40.900
We'll drop anchor there!

1168
01:45:42.861 --> 01:45:44.380
We can't land there!

1169
01:45:44.381 --> 01:45:46.260
It's the island of the Sun God!

1170
01:45:46.261 --> 01:45:47.261
We need repairs!

1171
01:45:49.020 --> 01:45:50.620
These men are exhausted!

1172
01:45:50.621 --> 01:45:52.540
We've lost six of our own!

1173
01:45:52.541 --> 01:45:55.420
We'll drop anchor and take a break on land.

1174
01:45:55.421 --> 01:45:57.035
You're all going to die!

1175
01:45:59.986 --> 01:46:02.146
You're asking me what Teiresias told me?

1176
01:46:02.937 --> 01:46:06.703
He said you'll all die
if you kill

1177
01:46:07.227 --> 01:46:10.346
the Sun God's cattle.
which graze on that island.

1178
01:46:10.440 --> 01:46:11.440
You're all going to die!

1179
01:46:15.717 --> 01:46:16.717
It's okay.

1180
01:46:17.758 --> 01:46:20.893
We'll drop anchor and cook
our own provisions.

1181
01:46:20.981 --> 01:46:24.015
We'll pick some fruit. bring some water.
and nothing else.

1182
01:46:25.452 --> 01:46:28.052
Everyone. swear to it.

1183
01:46:42.272 --> 01:46:42.881
See?

1184
01:46:44.273 --> 01:46:45.633
The wind blew us right here.

1185
01:46:46.700 --> 01:46:51.740
We'll take a break. repair the boat.
and set sail as soon as the wind changes.

1186
01:46:52.820 --> 01:46:53.820
Grab that rope.

1187
01:47:05.019 --> 01:47:06.779
Did the crew keep their oath?

1188
01:47:08.080 --> 01:47:10.920
Yes. but not for long.

1189
01:47:12.620 --> 01:47:14.380
But the wind never changed.

1190
01:47:14.620 --> 01:47:18.179
The strong breeze that allowed us to anchor
made it impossible…

1191
01:47:20.953 --> 01:47:22.593
prevented it from sailing away.

1192
01:47:26.880 --> 01:47:28.280
And we ran out of supplies.

1193
01:47:31.593 --> 01:47:32.593
Run away!

1194
01:48:34.935 --> 01:48:38.580
Did you think that if you ate quietly.
the Sun God wouldn't notice?

1195
01:48:38.630 --> 01:48:40.342
We thought you wouldn't notice.

1196
01:48:40.941 --> 01:48:42.340
I'm not the problem.

1197
01:48:42.567 --> 01:48:43.726
I wanted you to live.

1198
01:48:49.420 --> 01:48:50.940
Teiresias warned you.

1199
01:48:51.024 --> 01:48:51.863
Circe warned you.

1200
01:48:51.947 --> 01:48:53.066
The gods have warned you.

1201
01:48:53.980 --> 01:48:56.420
You gods do not speak a language
that we understand.

1202
01:48:56.741 --> 01:49:00.580
And who doesn't understand pain. blood. or death?

1203
01:49:01.336 --> 01:49:02.860
When you see your men dying.

1204
01:49:02.944 --> 01:49:04.940
Do you see this as a coincidence. or as
a consequence of their actions?

1205
01:49:04.941 --> 01:49:08.100
I see the consequences in this…
and my own failure.

1206
01:49:08.101 --> 01:49:10.900
What if their death isn't
your failure. but theirs?

1207
01:49:11.074 --> 01:49:12.074
I'm leading them.

1208
01:49:13.540 --> 01:49:15.220
I brought them here.

1209
01:49:15.401 --> 01:49:16.401
And who brought you here?

1210
01:49:17.910 --> 01:49:19.430
Why do you set yourself above the gods?

1211
01:49:19.514 --> 01:49:21.393
Why are you taking all the blame?

1212
01:49:22.180 --> 01:49:23.940
Why don't you want to go home?

1213
01:49:24.181 --> 01:49:25.300
What are you waiting for?

1214
01:49:27.020 --> 01:49:28.580
Finish it.

1215
01:49:29.780 --> 01:49:30.780
Show them a little compassion.

1216
01:49:30.781 --> 01:49:33.860
My mercy has led us
to this cursed island.

1217
01:49:33.861 --> 01:49:35.300
I told you what would happen.

1218
01:49:35.301 --> 01:49:36.301
"Yes." he said.

1219
01:49:37.020 --> 01:49:39.620
And we decided that death by drowning
is better than death by starvation.

1220
01:49:45.675 --> 01:49:48.020
All right. here's your chance.

1221
01:49:48.021 --> 01:49:50.540
The winds have died down!

1222
01:49:54.220 --> 01:49:57.780
That silence was a trap meant to
draw us into Poseidon’s embrace.

1223
01:49:59.300 --> 01:50:00.460
under the sky of Zeus.

1224
01:50:32.380 --> 01:50:34.420
We're going to die!

1225
01:50:43.821 --> 01:50:45.140
Face the wind!

1226
01:50:45.223 --> 01:50:46.603
Against the wind!

1227
01:50:49.824 --> 01:50:51.184
Odysseus!

1228
01:50:52.701 --> 01:50:54.660
Get out of the way!

1229
01:51:07.700 --> 01:51:10.220
Surrender yourselves to the gods!

1230
01:51:13.541 --> 01:51:15.580
Call on Poseidon!

1231
01:51:22.453 --> 01:51:24.893
Just ask the gods for forgiveness!

1232
01:51:37.045 --> 01:51:38.245
Watch out!

1233
01:51:52.820 --> 01:51:55.900
The last thing I remember
was Zeus's cruel punishment.

1234
01:52:07.889 --> 01:52:09.725
Dorycus!

1235
01:52:40.560 --> 01:52:42.380
I don't know what happened to my men.

1236
01:52:42.381 --> 01:52:43.381
You know.

1237
01:52:45.295 --> 01:52:47.299
Exactly what
I had warned them about happened.

1238
01:52:47.654 --> 01:52:49.813
The waves washed me
ashore on your coast.

1239
01:52:50.061 --> 01:52:54.540
The waves brought the wreckage of your ship to me.
without a single member of your crew.

1240
01:52:56.633 --> 01:52:58.833
Half-dead and unconscious.

1241
01:53:21.660 --> 01:53:27.213
I watched you wander
through days that turned into weeks.

1242
01:53:28.065 --> 01:53:30.580
I knew that the lotus
would ease your awakening.

1243
01:53:32.340 --> 01:53:34.940
So that your pain
is in your body. not in your mind.

1244
01:53:37.860 --> 01:53:38.860
And it worked.

1245
01:53:41.540 --> 01:53:42.940
You really enjoyed it.

1246
01:53:44.345 --> 01:53:46.300
And you ate it willingly.

1247
01:53:50.180 --> 01:53:51.732
And while I was taking care of you.

1248
01:53:51.867 --> 01:53:53.146
Thank you.

1249
01:53:53.501 --> 01:53:54.580
Your love has found its way into my heart.

1250
01:54:00.225 --> 01:54:01.700
How long have I been here?

1251
01:54:04.145 --> 01:54:06.860
I kept telling myself
that I wouldn't give you any more.

1252
01:54:06.861 --> 01:54:08.740
And that you're strong enough
to remember.

1253
01:54:08.741 --> 01:54:09.580
How long. Kalypso?

1254
01:54:09.581 --> 01:54:11.140
You were so happy.

1255
01:54:11.141 --> 01:54:11.980
Was I happy?

1256
01:54:11.981 --> 01:54:14.805
We were very happy for years.

1257
01:54:14.954 --> 01:54:15.954
Years?

1258
01:54:22.735 --> 01:54:23.820
Seven years.

1259
01:54:24.930 --> 01:54:28.474
You gave me a lotus
and kept me here.

1260
01:54:28.558 --> 01:54:30.176
I saved your life.

1261
01:54:31.101 --> 01:54:32.260
I healed your body.

1262
01:54:32.261 --> 01:54:35.140
But I didn't dare
to heal your mind.

1263
01:54:36.666 --> 01:54:37.826
You were happy.

1264
01:54:38.725 --> 01:54:41.860
But your heart was always somewhere else.

1265
01:54:43.333 --> 01:54:46.333
And it was only natural
that your mind eventually followed it.

1266
01:54:47.599 --> 01:54:48.959
How could you?

1267
01:54:49.101 --> 01:54:54.435
The lotus clouds the mind and robs you of your will.
but it does not change your true nature.

1268
01:54:54.854 --> 01:54:57.990
For some reason. he wasn't
ready to go home.

1269
01:54:58.074 --> 01:54:59.341
I'm ready now.

1270
01:55:00.933 --> 01:55:02.573
And I always knew it would be that way.

1271
01:55:03.393 --> 01:55:04.233
Now?

1272
01:55:04.374 --> 01:55:07.213
When I found you. you were holding something.

1273
01:55:07.381 --> 01:55:09.180
And for weeks on end. he clutched it tightly in his palm.

1274
01:55:10.060 --> 01:55:16.140
You used to call out. mostly "Athena."
but sometimes you called out another name as well.

1275
01:55:30.066 --> 01:55:31.537
Penelope.

1276
01:55:35.736 --> 01:55:36.972
Help me get back home.

1277
01:55:37.780 --> 01:55:38.780
I'll help.

1278
01:55:40.226 --> 01:55:42.426
You're a man who wants to
be in control of your own destiny.

1279
01:55:44.072 --> 01:55:45.592
But you can't control that.

1280
01:55:49.000 --> 01:55:53.681
You must surrender to the storm.
surrender to Poseidon.

1281
01:55:54.920 --> 01:55:56.200
and accept his punishment.

1282
01:55:59.306 --> 01:56:00.706
Zeus won't let him kill you.

1283
01:56:04.413 --> 01:56:07.413
Surrender to fate. let go of control.
and live.

1284
01:56:10.820 --> 01:56:14.540
It's a leap of faith you've
never dared to take. Odysseus.

1285
01:57:06.532 --> 01:57:07.920
Odysseus.

1286
01:57:15.785 --> 01:57:16.785
Where am I?

1287
01:57:18.953 --> 01:57:20.753
Don't you recognize your home?

1288
01:58:00.326 --> 01:58:01.926
Zeus welcomes you. strangers.

1289
01:58:03.086 --> 01:58:03.940
How many of you are there?

1290
01:58:04.739 --> 01:58:05.500
Half a dozen.

1291
01:58:06.423 --> 01:58:07.348
Would you like some food?

1292
01:58:07.439 --> 01:58:08.658
And some snacks for the trip.

1293
01:58:09.426 --> 01:58:10.586
Where are you going?

1294
01:58:10.981 --> 01:58:12.030
We need pigs.

1295
01:58:12.230 --> 01:58:15.119
We're continuing on to Pyl.

1296
01:58:15.661 --> 01:58:16.700
Are you pilgrims?

1297
01:58:17.813 --> 01:58:19.493
Do we look like pilgrims?

1298
01:58:19.614 --> 01:58:20.693
My eyesight is poor.

1299
01:58:21.710 --> 01:58:23.161
Zeus commands me to feed you.

1300
01:58:23.361 --> 01:58:25.580
But the pigs aren't mine.
so I can't sell them.

1301
01:58:25.581 --> 01:58:26.845
Don't worry.

1302
01:58:27.527 --> 01:58:28.527
We have no intention of paying.

1303
01:58:31.900 --> 01:58:33.899
Please give my regards to your mother.

1304
01:58:37.406 --> 01:58:39.046
And tell her I'm sorry.

1305
01:58:39.130 --> 01:58:40.520
I'm sorry about what happened in Troy

1306
01:58:40.604 --> 01:58:42.754
and everything that happened in my name.

1307
01:58:49.020 --> 01:58:51.300
I'm sorry I haven't heard
from your father.

1308
01:58:51.384 --> 01:58:52.800
But you have messages.

1309
01:58:53.187 --> 01:58:54.986
It's not about where he is.
but about who he is.

1310
01:58:56.573 --> 01:58:58.808
And now. more than ever before. I hope
that one day I'll meet him.

1311
01:59:04.240 --> 01:59:06.160
Where are your dogs. old man?

1312
01:59:10.820 --> 01:59:12.425
I'm burning their bodies.

1313
01:59:17.205 --> 01:59:19.760
Can Zeus offer you some food. stranger?

1314
01:59:20.790 --> 01:59:21.939
Have you been attacked?

1315
01:59:22.454 --> 01:59:24.373
The dog saved the shepherds on your farm.

1316
01:59:24.457 --> 01:59:26.616
No. we burned his brothers first.

1317
01:59:29.830 --> 01:59:31.283
What happened to Ithaca?

1318
01:59:31.767 --> 01:59:33.006
Darkness.

1319
01:59:33.170 --> 01:59:35.709
Dia's Law is in ruins.

1320
01:59:36.321 --> 01:59:39.720
A kingdom without a king.
ever since my lord died.

1321
01:59:39.961 --> 01:59:40.961
Your master?

1322
01:59:41.686 --> 01:59:42.908
Odysseus.

1323
01:59:43.800 --> 01:59:45.240
The Hero of Troy.

1324
01:59:47.680 --> 01:59:49.440
But Odysseus is alive.

1325
01:59:49.561 --> 01:59:51.300
False hopes.

1326
01:59:51.384 --> 01:59:53.503
Enough. enough!

1327
01:59:53.681 --> 01:59:57.422
Enough. enough. enough!

1328
01:59:57.506 --> 02:00:00.424
Telemachus. Telemachus. Telemachus.

1329
02:00:00.827 --> 02:00:01.666
Telemachus?

1330
02:00:01.750 --> 02:00:03.109
He's in danger.

1331
02:00:03.250 --> 02:00:05.520
He's in danger.
and I can't help him.

1332
02:00:05.521 --> 02:00:09.080
There must be others on Ithaca. too.
who are willing to help Odysseus's son.

1333
02:00:09.081 --> 02:00:11.322
The palace is under the control of the suitors.

1334
02:00:12.011 --> 02:00:14.466
No one is loyal to Odysseus anymore.

1335
02:00:15.800 --> 02:00:16.900
And the queen?

1336
02:00:17.770 --> 02:00:19.960
Beautiful and wise Penelope.

1337
02:00:20.890 --> 02:00:25.480
She is being held hostage by the worst kind of men.
and she will soon have to get married.

1338
02:00:25.481 --> 02:00:28.080
What can she do without her son?

1339
02:00:28.081 --> 02:00:29.081
Please write something.

1340
02:00:34.290 --> 02:00:35.855
What danger does Telemachus face?

1341
02:00:36.703 --> 02:00:38.513
He's on his way back from Sparta.

1342
02:00:40.130 --> 02:00:44.433
The suitors sent bandits to Pyl
to set a trap for him in the temple.

1343
02:00:45.595 --> 02:00:46.960
Give me some provisions for the trip.

1344
02:00:47.249 --> 02:00:50.564
You'll never catch up to the bandits.

1345
02:00:50.648 --> 02:00:53.839
If I get to Pyl before Telemachus.
I can warn him.

1346
02:00:54.410 --> 02:00:55.798
Why do you help?

1347
02:00:56.730 --> 02:00:58.524
Promise me you won't get mad when I tell you this

1348
02:00:59.161 --> 02:01:01.160
I served with Odysseus at Troy.

1349
02:01:01.161 --> 02:01:02.161
Really?

1350
02:01:03.450 --> 02:01:04.840
He was a strict commander.

1351
02:01:04.841 --> 02:01:06.120
But he was fair.

1352
02:01:07.695 --> 02:01:09.680
Righteous and worthy of emulation.

1353
02:01:11.120 --> 02:01:12.120
You liked him.

1354
02:01:13.040 --> 02:01:14.040
Like a son.

1355
02:01:14.819 --> 02:01:17.639
Well. he didn't die.

1356
02:01:17.723 --> 02:01:20.313
Will he come home?

1357
02:01:20.397 --> 02:01:21.385
Soon.

1358
02:01:22.190 --> 02:01:23.880
To take revenge.

1359
02:01:25.735 --> 02:01:27.040
At full strength.

1360
02:01:36.560 --> 02:01:39.452
I'll do the talking.
No one must know who you are.

1361
02:01:39.536 --> 02:01:41.304
Maybe we shouldn't have hired a security guard.

1362
02:01:41.721 --> 02:01:43.965
If we encounter danger along the way.
we'll thank God that we have it.

1363
02:01:55.758 --> 02:01:56.909
Get moving!

1364
02:01:56.993 --> 02:01:58.393
I have a message.

1365
02:01:58.477 --> 02:01:59.477
Wait!

1366
02:02:00.720 --> 02:02:01.920
Eumaeus sent me!

1367
02:02:04.133 --> 02:02:05.133
Find that beggar.

1368
02:02:21.387 --> 02:02:22.906
Your men will wait outside.

1369
02:02:22.990 --> 02:02:23.990
Weapons. too.

1370
02:02:41.150 --> 02:02:42.689
Zeus welcomes you. strangers.

1371
02:02:43.087 --> 02:02:44.766
Did you come from far away?

1372
02:02:44.850 --> 02:02:45.850
Not really.

1373
02:02:47.261 --> 02:02:49.086
Did you come here to relax for the night?

1374
02:02:51.347 --> 02:02:53.427
We have come to offer a sacrifice to Athena.

1375
02:02:55.553 --> 02:02:58.713
And we will generously reward you
for your hospitality.

1376
02:02:58.797 --> 02:02:59.836
Of course.

1377
02:03:00.001 --> 02:03:01.240
Take that beggar away.

1378
02:03:01.241 --> 02:03:02.640
He has a message for me.

1379
02:03:03.768 --> 02:03:04.913
Did Eumaeus send you?

1380
02:03:05.900 --> 02:03:06.900
Yes. sir.

1381
02:03:13.106 --> 02:03:14.466
Okay. what's the link?

1382
02:03:15.446 --> 02:03:16.766
It's personal.

1383
02:03:16.920 --> 02:03:17.920
These men…

1384
02:03:19.858 --> 02:03:21.053
They are priests.

1385
02:03:38.426 --> 02:03:40.889
This is your chance to leave. old man.

1386
02:03:41.434 --> 02:03:42.434
Go away. stranger.

1387
02:03:44.967 --> 02:03:47.174
I've already spared one old man this week.

1388
02:03:47.647 --> 02:03:48.876
But he was blind.

1389
02:03:49.567 --> 02:03:50.966
But I won't spare this one.

1390
02:04:29.446 --> 02:04:31.092
You held your own pretty well for a beggar.

1391
02:04:31.434 --> 02:04:33.473
No one is born a beggar.

1392
02:04:33.627 --> 02:04:35.346
Let's take this priest's cloak.

1393
02:04:35.430 --> 02:04:36.669
Throw it over your armor.

1394
02:04:38.853 --> 02:04:40.133
Help me carry his body away.

1395
02:04:40.217 --> 02:04:41.655
We have to leave him here.

1396
02:04:41.774 --> 02:04:42.613
No.

1397
02:04:42.721 --> 02:04:43.895
We have to get out of here.

1398
02:04:44.254 --> 02:04:45.653
That's no way to honor the dead.

1399
02:04:45.737 --> 02:04:47.001
That's how you stay alive.

1400
02:04:47.085 --> 02:04:48.364
He was my teacher.

1401
02:04:49.726 --> 02:04:51.062
Perhaps she took on the form of Athena.

1402
02:04:51.146 --> 02:04:52.655
His name was Mentor.

1403
02:04:53.147 --> 02:04:58.066
And once you're safe. we'll head west
into the unknown to honor him.

1404
02:04:58.150 --> 02:05:00.101
These men were sent by the suitors.

1405
02:05:00.185 --> 02:05:02.504
Others are coming; we have to leave.

1406
02:05:02.588 --> 02:05:03.467
At night?

1407
02:05:03.551 --> 02:05:05.062
It's safer than staying here.

1408
02:05:05.146 --> 02:05:07.695
And Mentor rests in the heart
of Athens' oldest temple.

1409
02:05:08.141 --> 02:05:10.300
For now. let's honor him with a fire.

1410
02:05:20.028 --> 02:05:21.632
Fire!

1411
02:05:25.488 --> 02:05:29.039
Fire! Fire!

1412
02:05:49.180 --> 02:05:51.140
How could a beggar have known Mentor?

1413
02:05:51.141 --> 02:05:52.300
I told you.

1414
02:05:52.301 --> 02:05:53.598
I wasn't always a beggar.

1415
02:05:54.101 --> 02:05:55.518
There is wisdom in your eyes.

1416
02:05:56.181 --> 02:05:57.020
Eyes like Athena's.

1417
02:05:57.021 --> 02:05:58.780
Don't look for gods in people.

1418
02:05:58.781 --> 02:06:00.540
You'll just end up disappointed.

1419
02:06:00.541 --> 02:06:02.180
So tell me. who are you?

1420
02:06:03.875 --> 02:06:05.182
My name is Sinon.

1421
02:06:05.925 --> 02:06:08.780
An old soldier who fought
under your father's command at Troy.

1422
02:06:10.090 --> 02:06:11.311
Did you know my father?

1423
02:06:11.621 --> 02:06:14.524
I took orders from him for longer
than I'd care to remember.

1424
02:06:15.780 --> 02:06:16.780
Wait. is he alive?

1425
02:06:18.060 --> 02:06:19.060
He's alive.

1426
02:06:20.660 --> 02:06:21.957
So where is he?

1427
02:06:22.261 --> 02:06:23.940
It's nearby.

1428
02:06:23.941 --> 02:06:26.380
He sent me ahead
to assess the situation.

1429
02:06:27.779 --> 02:06:29.579
My mother won't believe it.

1430
02:06:29.821 --> 02:06:31.060
No. you can't tell her that.

1431
02:06:32.340 --> 02:06:33.340
What. nothing?

1432
02:06:34.405 --> 02:06:35.420
Tell her.

1433
02:06:36.780 --> 02:06:39.020
Tell her that you've decided
it's time for her to get married again.

1434
02:06:40.980 --> 02:06:42.340
He doesn't have to test my mother.

1435
02:06:42.341 --> 02:06:43.540
How can he suspect her?

1436
02:06:43.541 --> 02:06:44.542
He doesn't suspect her.

1437
02:06:45.101 --> 02:06:47.460
But she has every reason
to forget him.

1438
02:06:49.275 --> 02:06:50.860
Are there any faithful servants here?

1439
02:06:51.940 --> 02:06:52.619
A few.

1440
02:06:53.820 --> 02:06:55.780
One has just proven his loyalty.

1441
02:06:57.340 --> 02:06:59.550
We need to gather the suitors at the palace.

1442
02:07:00.341 --> 02:07:01.690
That will never be a problem.

1443
02:07:02.301 --> 02:07:03.896
Do you have any gifts from Menelaus?

1444
02:07:04.341 --> 02:07:05.402
Yes. on my boat.

1445
02:07:05.941 --> 02:07:06.782
Okay.

1446
02:07:07.381 --> 02:07:08.563
Come back as a prince.

1447
02:07:08.981 --> 02:07:10.185
Throw a party.

1448
02:07:10.741 --> 02:07:11.741
And I'll be there.

1449
02:07:12.695 --> 02:07:14.272
What are you going to do at the reception?

1450
02:07:14.941 --> 02:07:15.912
To beg.

1451
02:07:16.501 --> 02:07:18.580
The best view of a person is from below.

1452
02:07:20.580 --> 02:07:21.940
How many suitors are there?

1453
02:07:22.780 --> 02:07:23.647
Tens.

1454
02:07:23.647 --> 02:07:24.647
Tens.

1455
02:07:24.701 --> 02:07:27.700
If all his men are like you.
then he doesn't really have any.

1456
02:07:28.380 --> 02:07:29.500
What. are you alone?

1457
02:07:30.820 --> 02:07:31.820
You. too.

1458
02:07:37.900 --> 02:07:39.100
Telemachus has returned.

1459
02:07:39.101 --> 02:07:39.940
What?

1460
02:07:39.941 --> 02:07:41.340
Telemachus has returned.

1461
02:07:41.341 --> 02:07:42.580
Who says that?

1462
02:07:42.581 --> 02:07:44.340
Everyone says that.

1463
02:07:44.341 --> 02:07:46.020
Where is Telemachus?

1464
02:07:46.021 --> 02:07:47.060
My queen!

1465
02:07:47.061 --> 02:07:49.060
Your place is definitely up there.

1466
02:07:57.234 --> 02:07:57.951
Is that true?

1467
02:08:00.980 --> 02:08:01.980
You've come home.

1468
02:08:02.846 --> 02:08:03.916
I'm back.

1469
02:08:04.501 --> 02:08:06.228
I was thinking we could
celebrate it tonight.

1470
02:08:07.021 --> 02:08:09.900
If we have something to celebrate.

1471
02:08:11.500 --> 02:08:12.537
Don't worry.

1472
02:08:12.981 --> 02:08:16.024
My valiant suitors will shower us
with gifts in honor of your return.

1473
02:08:16.141 --> 02:08:17.900
I'm really glad
that you came back safe and sound.

1474
02:08:28.780 --> 02:08:30.308
It's good to see you back on your feet.

1475
02:08:30.741 --> 02:08:33.100
It's time to take you to the palace.

1476
02:08:36.460 --> 02:08:38.260
What did you find out about your father?

1477
02:08:39.860 --> 02:08:40.860
Nothing.

1478
02:08:41.715 --> 02:08:45.350
Stories from the war. but no one knows
what became of him after Troy.

1479
02:08:45.861 --> 02:08:46.861
I'm sorry. Mom.

1480
02:08:47.940 --> 02:08:51.580
In Sparta. everyone is talking
about the impending disaster.

1481
02:08:52.460 --> 02:08:56.460
About the Age of Darkness brought about by the Sea
Nations. who have broken the law of Dia.

1482
02:08:56.461 --> 02:08:57.820
Those stories are true.

1483
02:08:57.821 --> 02:08:59.812
Our civilization is crumbling.

1484
02:09:00.101 --> 02:09:03.159
Menelaus fears that we cannot raise
an army while the throne is vacant.

1485
02:09:03.541 --> 02:09:04.860
That's why we came up with a plan.

1486
02:09:06.540 --> 02:09:07.540
What's the plan?

1487
02:09:09.700 --> 02:09:11.780
The only way
to get suitors to leave.

1488
02:09:14.030 --> 02:09:15.380
It's time for you to make a choice.

1489
02:09:19.470 --> 02:09:21.100
Do you want me to get married again?

1490
02:09:21.101 --> 02:09:24.020
If you get married.
the other suitors will leave.

1491
02:09:24.021 --> 02:09:26.060
and then I'll have to
face only one of them.

1492
02:09:26.061 --> 02:09:26.980
I'm getting closer to adulthood.

1493
02:09:26.981 --> 02:09:30.386
I'll tell the elders that I
learned of Odysseus's death in Sparta.

1494
02:09:30.821 --> 02:09:34.060
Menelaus will support me.
and they will allow me to take the throne.

1495
02:09:34.061 --> 02:09:37.620
So you'll renounce your father. and I'll leave
my home to live with the serpent?

1496
02:09:37.621 --> 02:09:38.526
Is that your plan?

1497
02:09:38.880 --> 02:09:40.300
Do you have a better one?

1498
02:09:42.980 --> 02:09:46.300
Lock the door. light a fire.
and burn those good-for-nothings alive.

1499
02:09:47.355 --> 02:09:51.100
Do you think that if I get married.
life will go back to the way it was?

1500
02:09:52.075 --> 02:09:55.260
That world has ended.
And in this world

1501
02:09:55.261 --> 02:09:58.580
They won't hand over power
to you or to Odysseus.

1502
02:09:58.581 --> 02:10:00.343
And if he were to return.
he would have to kill them.

1503
02:10:01.101 --> 02:10:02.101
Just like you.

1504
02:10:02.940 --> 02:10:05.940
If you kill them.
their families will seek revenge.

1505
02:10:05.941 --> 02:10:07.180
And they'll kick me out.

1506
02:10:08.250 --> 02:10:10.100
And without me. the throne will be lost.

1507
02:10:10.101 --> 02:10:11.860
Because only men can fill that position.

1508
02:10:13.740 --> 02:10:14.860
An Empty Throne?

1509
02:10:16.195 --> 02:10:19.455
I sat on that empty throne
for a full twenty years!

1510
02:10:19.915 --> 02:10:22.980
My experience and my years of service.

1511
02:10:22.981 --> 02:10:25.367
None of that is worth the few hairs
that have grown on your chin.

1512
02:10:28.340 --> 02:10:29.500
Do you want peace?

1513
02:10:30.780 --> 02:10:31.620
Or do you want revenge?

1514
02:10:31.621 --> 02:10:33.140
I want an Odyssey!

1515
02:10:36.020 --> 02:10:39.140
Sing. Muse. the story of a single face.

1516
02:10:42.620 --> 02:10:43.620
One face.

1517
02:10:48.220 --> 02:10:49.220
One fleet.

1518
02:10:53.700 --> 02:10:54.700
One war.

1519
02:10:58.980 --> 02:10:59.980
in Troy…

1520
02:11:04.220 --> 02:11:05.980
Sing. Muse. the story of one man.

1521
02:11:10.297 --> 02:11:11.219
One man!

1522
02:11:15.887 --> 02:11:16.887
One idea!

1523
02:11:20.573 --> 02:11:21.573
What a trick!

1524
02:11:25.746 --> 02:11:26.826
Eumaeus. come on in.

1525
02:11:34.146 --> 02:11:35.146
Argos.

1526
02:11:57.810 --> 02:11:59.820
You have to bury him at the top of the cliff.

1527
02:12:01.810 --> 02:12:02.810
Where did you meet?

1528
02:12:11.879 --> 02:12:12.879
You are Odysseus.

1529
02:12:16.230 --> 02:12:17.230
You are my father.

1530
02:12:20.194 --> 02:12:21.194
I'm just a beggar.

1531
02:12:24.886 --> 02:12:25.886
And you are

1532
02:12:28.045 --> 02:12:33.495
the noble. handsome son of Odysseus.

1533
02:12:35.340 --> 02:12:38.373
who. when you finally meet him.

1534
02:12:40.746 --> 02:12:41.826
He'll be proud of you.

1535
02:12:46.300 --> 02:12:48.700
Welcome home. stranger.

1536
02:12:52.327 --> 02:12:53.850
Why the Darkness of Victory

1537
02:12:54.940 --> 02:12:56.820
envelops those who create myths.

1538
02:12:59.307 --> 02:13:01.707
and turns us into poets and blind beggars.

1539
02:13:09.420 --> 02:13:11.398
Please accept Dio's hospitality.

1540
02:13:11.874 --> 02:13:13.753
Give the stranger something to eat.

1541
02:13:13.861 --> 02:13:14.861
No. sir.

1542
02:13:15.700 --> 02:13:17.540
All I need is a bowl for my food.

1543
02:13:23.140 --> 02:13:25.820
Today we had to welcome
yet another filthy intruder.

1544
02:13:25.821 --> 02:13:27.780
What difference will
one more beggar make at this feast?

1545
02:13:45.940 --> 02:13:48.580
We have to kill him before he
regains the elders' favor.

1546
02:13:48.581 --> 02:13:49.581
How?

1547
02:14:04.809 --> 02:14:06.316
Bring on the wine!

1548
02:14:06.400 --> 02:14:09.480
Provoke him and make him grab a weapon.

1549
02:14:14.208 --> 02:14:16.700
He's no longer that reckless boy
we used to know.

1550
02:14:17.060 --> 02:14:19.779
When we get back tonight.
I'll cut out his tongue.

1551
02:14:20.380 --> 02:14:21.900
Now get to work. old man.

1552
02:14:21.901 --> 02:14:24.180
Please. I haven't eaten all day.

1553
02:14:24.181 --> 02:14:25.420
I said. "Get out!"

1554
02:14:25.421 --> 02:14:28.580
You have no right to withhold food
that does not belong to you.

1555
02:14:30.741 --> 02:14:32.537
There's no room in your bowl.

1556
02:14:32.621 --> 02:14:33.621
It'll fit.

1557
02:14:35.340 --> 02:14:36.340
You're welcome.

1558
02:14:39.673 --> 02:14:40.673
It really does fit.

1559
02:14:42.260 --> 02:14:43.260
Here you go.

1560
02:14:47.603 --> 02:14:49.443
I'm guessing you want
something from me now.

1561
02:14:49.527 --> 02:14:52.726
No. actually. I have something for you.

1562
02:14:53.819 --> 02:14:55.259
Do you have something for me?

1563
02:14:56.353 --> 02:14:57.793
Do you have anything for me?

1564
02:14:59.675 --> 02:15:05.565
And what could a filthy beggar
like you possibly have that I would want?

1565
02:15:06.234 --> 02:15:08.433
Actually. it's yours.

1566
02:15:14.213 --> 02:15:15.573
I was asked
to return this to you.

1567
02:15:18.200 --> 02:15:19.200
That's not mine.

1568
02:15:20.820 --> 02:15:21.704
No?

1569
02:15:25.140 --> 02:15:26.580
So where did you get that from?

1570
02:15:28.820 --> 02:15:30.540
Don't turn your back on me!

1571
02:15:36.279 --> 02:15:39.107
Antinous. you can't
treat a beggar like that.

1572
02:15:39.191 --> 02:15:40.391
It is Dia's law.

1573
02:15:41.061 --> 02:15:42.100
The Law of Dia?

1574
02:15:46.861 --> 02:15:48.060
Do you think this is God?

1575
02:15:51.740 --> 02:15:53.420
Do you think he's some kind of
god in disguise?

1576
02:15:54.500 --> 02:15:55.500
Are you?

1577
02:15:57.260 --> 02:15:58.260
Are you God?

1578
02:15:59.660 --> 02:16:00.660
An intruder?

1579
02:16:01.695 --> 02:16:02.695
No.

1580
02:16:03.580 --> 02:16:06.140
I am a veteran of the Trojan War.

1581
02:16:06.141 --> 02:16:08.500
A veteran of the Trojan War.

1582
02:16:10.941 --> 02:16:12.460
So. what's your name. veteran?

1583
02:16:16.719 --> 02:16:17.719
Sinon.

1584
02:16:21.305 --> 02:16:23.220
Does that name ring a bell?

1585
02:16:24.260 --> 02:16:25.260
No.

1586
02:16:26.500 --> 02:16:30.405
I adopted it in honor of the bravest
boy I ever knew.

1587
02:16:30.861 --> 02:16:31.861
Otherwise?

1588
02:16:33.530 --> 02:16:34.980
Wasn't that your shepherd's son?

1589
02:16:37.695 --> 02:16:38.860
I don't remember.

1590
02:16:38.861 --> 02:16:41.507
Yes. yes.
you wanted him to take your place.

1591
02:16:42.061 --> 02:16:43.339
Where did you find that?

1592
02:16:43.901 --> 02:16:45.060
That's where you'll be soon. too.

1593
02:16:46.300 --> 02:16:47.300
And where is that?

1594
02:16:48.205 --> 02:16:49.220
In Hád.

1595
02:16:52.767 --> 02:16:53.767
Enough is enough!

1596
02:16:55.579 --> 02:16:57.284
The party is over!

1597
02:17:01.419 --> 02:17:02.419
Bastards.

1598
02:17:04.053 --> 02:17:05.053
That's not mine!

1599
02:17:13.900 --> 02:17:15.860
The queen would like to speak with you.

1600
02:17:15.861 --> 02:17:18.259
Wait until they leave. then start a fire.

1601
02:18:09.350 --> 02:18:10.350
Welcome. traveler.

1602
02:18:11.700 --> 02:18:13.100
My dear lady.

1603
02:18:13.101 --> 02:18:16.487
Melanthó. wash the stranger's feet.

1604
02:18:17.021 --> 02:18:18.300
That's not necessary.

1605
02:18:18.301 --> 02:18:22.065
My husband insisted that we
treat strangers as we would our own family.

1606
02:18:22.501 --> 02:18:25.020
Do you know Odysseus. my husband?

1607
02:18:25.221 --> 02:18:26.253
Of course.

1608
02:18:26.741 --> 02:18:27.838
And how do you know him?

1609
02:18:28.541 --> 02:18:29.820
I was in Troy. my lady.

1610
02:18:30.790 --> 02:18:32.579
And maybe these songs
seem funny to you.

1611
02:18:34.300 --> 02:18:35.300
They make me cry.

1612
02:18:36.740 --> 02:18:37.392
Why?

1613
02:18:38.479 --> 02:18:39.620
For what has been lost.

1614
02:18:40.659 --> 02:18:41.659
Souls?

1615
02:18:42.579 --> 02:18:43.579
And years.

1616
02:18:45.060 --> 02:18:46.060
Everything.

1617
02:18:53.780 --> 02:18:55.380
Did you know about Odysseus in Troy?

1618
02:18:56.093 --> 02:18:57.093
Yes.

1619
02:18:58.204 --> 02:19:01.140
Was there anything unusual
about his clothing?

1620
02:19:01.214 --> 02:19:02.387
Something he used to wear?

1621
02:19:02.989 --> 02:19:04.540
He was wearing an unusual brooch.

1622
02:19:05.339 --> 02:19:06.339
Unusual?

1623
02:19:07.270 --> 02:19:10.780
She was beautiful. but she seemed out of place.

1624
02:19:10.781 --> 02:19:11.781
Describe her to me.

1625
02:19:26.100 --> 02:19:27.380
I'll bring some more water.

1626
02:19:32.184 --> 02:19:34.579
It was a beautiful gold brooch.

1627
02:19:35.959 --> 02:19:38.659
In the center. it took the form of Athena.
surrounded by vine leaves.

1628
02:19:47.629 --> 02:19:48.740
Did he wear it often?

1629
02:19:50.580 --> 02:19:51.780
He never took it off.

1630
02:20:12.069 --> 02:20:14.980
How come you came back home.
but he didn't?

1631
02:20:16.110 --> 02:20:17.291
I haven't come back yet.

1632
02:20:17.741 --> 02:20:18.741
Not yet.

1633
02:20:19.719 --> 02:20:20.441
Why?

1634
02:20:21.219 --> 02:20:23.460
Coming home isn't always easy.

1635
02:20:24.469 --> 02:20:26.620
That goes for Odysseus
just as much as it does for me.

1636
02:20:28.449 --> 02:20:35.620
The Odysseus I knew would have found
his way to me. or he would have fought his way there.

1637
02:20:36.670 --> 02:20:39.860
What if the Odysseus you knew
had lost his way?

1638
02:20:44.629 --> 02:20:50.294
What if one night
in a strange city

1639
02:20:52.389 --> 02:20:57.389
He saw something that convinced him
that the home he knew.

1640
02:20:58.939 --> 02:21:00.740
Does it no longer exist?

1641
02:21:04.939 --> 02:21:08.260
What if. when he emerged from the horse's belly

1642
02:21:12.740 --> 02:21:14.340
and opened the gates of Troy.

1643
02:21:20.020 --> 02:21:25.896
Did he see the fury of ten years. which
came crashing down on that city in a single night?

1644
02:22:13.460 --> 02:22:14.780
Everyone. come on. now!

1645
02:23:41.620 --> 02:23:50.037
We left them a gift. a symbol of peace.
and they welcomed it into their home.

1646
02:24:00.660 --> 02:24:06.262
We have violated everything
that is sacred among people.

1647
02:24:08.420 --> 02:24:14.820
And we turned the fight into a hunt.

1648
02:24:19.706 --> 02:24:21.060
No. please.

1649
02:24:30.566 --> 02:24:35.486
The burning of the walls of Troy meant
the burning of the entire world.

1650
02:24:37.345 --> 02:24:38.860
including his home.

1651
02:24:45.875 --> 02:24:50.122
What if he knew exactly
that very night.

1652
02:24:53.340 --> 02:24:57.500
walking through the flames of anarchy and pain…

1653
02:25:46.740 --> 02:25:50.040
What if he knew. in the days that followed.
amid the boisterous celebrations.

1654
02:25:56.541 --> 02:25:59.860
What exactly did he do?

1655
02:26:18.153 --> 02:26:20.073
And what did all of you do?

1656
02:26:20.461 --> 02:26:21.780
One man's idea.

1657
02:26:23.620 --> 02:26:29.173
And the deceit of one man.
which forever broke the law of Dia.

1658
02:26:30.635 --> 02:26:33.700
We lived in a world of palaces and commerce.

1659
02:26:35.740 --> 02:26:40.740
and our language was unable to capture its beauty.
until we destroyed it.

1660
02:26:43.505 --> 02:26:45.700
Are you those Maritime Nations?

1661
02:26:48.314 --> 02:26:49.314
Yes. my queen.

1662
02:26:51.050 --> 02:26:54.226
Violations of the Dia Act
are spreading like the plague.

1663
02:26:54.661 --> 02:26:58.860
The Bronze Age is crumbling. and perhaps he
couldn't bear the sight of the ruins

1664
02:26:58.861 --> 02:27:04.161
what happened anywhere.
especially in his own home.

1665
02:27:13.985 --> 02:27:15.460
But Odysseus is alive

1666
02:27:17.745 --> 02:27:19.860
and it will be there
when you need it most.

1667
02:27:21.780 --> 02:27:26.780
My God. I've been living among its ruins
for quite a long time now.

1668
02:27:32.780 --> 02:27:34.340
Bring the suitors at dawn.

1669
02:27:34.341 --> 02:27:36.220
And take the weapons out of the hall.

1670
02:27:36.221 --> 02:27:38.333
In a few hours. one suitor will be happy.

1671
02:27:38.533 --> 02:27:40.860
while the others will fly into a rage
and demand revenge.

1672
02:27:40.861 --> 02:27:42.658
They'll provoke you.
but there won't be any weapons at hand.

1673
02:27:42.659 --> 02:27:45.125
During the exam.
the doors will be locked.

1674
02:27:45.701 --> 02:27:47.900
I will marry the one
who passes my test.

1675
02:27:54.701 --> 02:27:56.380
How do we deal with suitors?

1676
02:27:57.265 --> 02:27:58.265
Not us.

1677
02:27:59.025 --> 02:28:02.980
Their blood will be on my hands.
or you'll face exile.

1678
02:28:04.065 --> 02:28:05.065
Anyway.

1679
02:28:06.140 --> 02:28:08.082
Just make sure
they don't get their hands on any weapons.

1680
02:28:08.507 --> 02:28:09.507
I'll take care of that.

1681
02:28:10.500 --> 02:28:12.416
Even with just one weapon.
there are too many of them.

1682
02:28:13.301 --> 02:28:14.700
And you won't have a gun.

1683
02:28:16.395 --> 02:28:17.660
I'll have a gun.

1684
02:28:19.435 --> 02:28:21.020
Your mother took care of it.

1685
02:28:23.410 --> 02:28:25.380
Call together the servants you trust.

1686
02:28:25.381 --> 02:28:27.476
And lock the weapons in the upper compartment.

1687
02:28:27.632 --> 02:28:29.213
Yes. yes. yes. yes.

1688
02:28:46.740 --> 02:28:49.377
All right. all right. wait a minute.

1689
02:28:56.426 --> 02:28:58.722
All right. all right.

1690
02:29:20.526 --> 02:29:22.566
It seems like he's only interested in the result.

1691
02:29:22.901 --> 02:29:24.100
What will the exam be like?

1692
02:29:48.656 --> 02:29:52.656
For many years. while my husband was away.

1693
02:29:53.541 --> 02:29:56.180
You took over this palace
and feasted there.

1694
02:29:56.834 --> 02:30:00.033
And your excuse was
that you wanted to marry me.

1695
02:30:03.853 --> 02:30:08.342
And while I was listening
to your celebrations. your arguments.

1696
02:30:10.183 --> 02:30:13.700
your mistreatment of my servants
and your disrespect for my house.

1697
02:30:13.701 --> 02:30:18.660
I wanted nothing more than for Odysseus
to return and see it all.

1698
02:30:25.460 --> 02:30:30.385
But last night I realized
that he isn't here.

1699
02:30:33.753 --> 02:30:34.873
He'll never come back.

1700
02:30:36.947 --> 02:30:41.947
That's why I'm ready to marry
any man.

1701
02:30:42.180 --> 02:30:47.220
who can draw this bow and shoot
an arrow through twelve axes.

1702
02:30:49.300 --> 02:30:50.300
Get out.

1703
02:30:52.300 --> 02:30:53.620
Show me who you are.

1704
02:30:55.220 --> 02:30:56.220
Eumaeus.

1705
02:31:07.940 --> 02:31:09.540
Who will be first?

1706
02:31:09.714 --> 02:31:10.714
Give me that bow.

1707
02:31:31.293 --> 02:31:32.573
That's impossible.

1708
02:31:32.657 --> 02:31:34.056
You!

1709
02:31:34.181 --> 02:31:36.252
My lord. Odysseus did it with ease.

1710
02:31:36.701 --> 02:31:37.701
Then you do it.

1711
02:31:38.415 --> 02:31:39.540
I'll wait until it's my turn.

1712
02:31:40.620 --> 02:31:42.982
What if we heated the bow
and oiled it?

1713
02:31:44.330 --> 02:31:46.422
This bow hasn't been drawn in years.

1714
02:32:19.581 --> 02:32:20.446
It's your turn.

1715
02:32:21.127 --> 02:32:22.127
No need.

1716
02:32:25.415 --> 02:32:26.776
My queen said what she wanted to say.

1717
02:32:27.314 --> 02:32:29.113
We cannot take Odysseus's place.

1718
02:32:29.267 --> 02:32:30.786
Some of us have always known that.

1719
02:32:30.901 --> 02:32:34.901
And others would have followed that great man
to Troy. if he had allowed us to.

1720
02:32:37.695 --> 02:32:39.220
But he never came back.

1721
02:32:41.195 --> 02:32:42.740
So choose the best
man among us.

1722
02:32:44.595 --> 02:32:46.643
We all failed your test.

1723
02:32:47.301 --> 02:32:48.301
Not me.

1724
02:32:53.166 --> 02:32:55.126
Someone should take those beggars away.

1725
02:32:56.181 --> 02:32:58.980
The door is locked
until the exam is over.

1726
02:32:58.981 --> 02:33:00.580
The exam is over.

1727
02:33:01.720 --> 02:33:03.040
I want to give it a try.

1728
02:33:03.141 --> 02:33:05.060
That's enough. old man.

1729
02:33:06.501 --> 02:33:08.020
He has the right to try.

1730
02:33:09.220 --> 02:33:11.576
And if you don't do it.
let him try.

1731
02:33:11.861 --> 02:33:13.900
You're making fun of your mother's wish.

1732
02:33:13.901 --> 02:33:16.580
My mother's wish is
for all of you to burn to ashes.

1733
02:33:27.900 --> 02:33:30.300
He's an old archer.

1734
02:33:33.593 --> 02:33:34.563
Hurry up!

1735
02:33:35.150 --> 02:33:37.226
Watch an expert at work.

1736
02:33:37.310 --> 02:33:38.149
Foot-licker!

1737
02:33:40.030 --> 02:33:41.137
Come on.

1738
02:33:41.221 --> 02:33:43.220
Old Man. forget about it.

1739
02:33:44.243 --> 02:33:45.701
Hey. man!

1740
02:33:45.784 --> 02:33:47.127
Hurry up!

1741
02:33:54.153 --> 02:33:56.873
All right. buddy. that's enough.

1742
02:33:56.981 --> 02:33:58.460
Leave him alone. Let's go.

1743
02:34:42.261 --> 02:34:43.667
You're Ody…

1744
02:34:50.220 --> 02:34:52.620
Your place really is down there.

1745
02:35:06.216 --> 02:35:07.403
Is that him?

1746
02:35:08.300 --> 02:35:09.300
That's him!

1747
02:35:19.019 --> 02:35:20.540
Guard the weapon.

1748
02:35:34.520 --> 02:35:36.040
Where are the weapons?

1749
02:35:36.041 --> 02:35:37.626
In stock upstairs.

1750
02:35:37.710 --> 02:35:38.829
Catch him!

1751
02:35:42.999 --> 02:35:44.519
What are you waiting for?

1752
02:35:44.603 --> 02:35:46.522
It's going to kill us all!

1753
02:35:49.780 --> 02:35:51.300
Mom. go outside!

1754
02:35:55.860 --> 02:35:58.540
I told you so. 
You're making a scene here. you lout.

1755
02:35:58.541 --> 02:36:00.432
No! No!

1756
02:36:00.917 --> 02:36:03.614
Eumaeus! Let me out!

1757
02:36:08.670 --> 02:36:09.795
Down!

1758
02:36:20.852 --> 02:36:21.924
No!

1759
02:37:17.340 --> 02:37:18.660
Lock the door!

1760
02:38:18.780 --> 02:38:20.451
Bring the shields. bring the shields!

1761
02:38:59.002 --> 02:39:00.150
Copies!

1762
02:39:30.279 --> 02:39:31.679
Use copies!

1763
02:40:25.573 --> 02:40:27.573
You thought you were going to kill us all.

1764
02:40:27.960 --> 02:40:29.356
I'll kill you. too.

1765
02:40:30.767 --> 02:40:31.838
Finish it.

1766
02:40:35.033 --> 02:40:36.033
Dad!

1767
02:41:06.340 --> 02:41:07.778
When you get to Hades.
tell my men.

1768
02:41:09.853 --> 02:41:13.423
that I will go west
and keep my oath.

1769
02:41:19.540 --> 02:41:25.349
And tell Sinon
that I have avenged your shame.

1770
02:41:45.039 --> 02:41:46.039
What happened?

1771
02:41:47.549 --> 02:41:48.549
Did he beat them?

1772
02:41:50.059 --> 02:41:51.059
Did he die?

1773
02:41:53.029 --> 02:41:55.500
We have severed the fragile bonds
between people.

1774
02:42:04.563 --> 02:42:06.754
And throughout the following
centuries of darkness

1775
02:42:07.559 --> 02:42:09.052
...stories about Troy…

1776
02:42:10.051 --> 02:42:11.572
…they will speak
only in songs.

1777
02:43:30.580 --> 02:43:32.468
Odysseus.

1778
02:43:33.905 --> 02:43:35.380
Yes. my love.

1779
02:43:36.615 --> 02:43:37.660
Odysseus.

1780
02:43:38.257 --> 02:43:40.790
My love. you cannot die.

1781
02:43:42.619 --> 02:43:45.272
Finally. at the end.

1782
02:43:47.110 --> 02:43:51.100
To see my beloved.
if I may. one last time.

1783
02:43:52.179 --> 02:43:55.700
No. no. no. no.
you can't. you can't.

1784
02:43:55.701 --> 02:43:57.838
Not now. not now. please.
please. it's over.

1785
02:44:00.266 --> 02:44:01.641
You can't die.

1786
02:44:02.990 --> 02:44:03.990
Not death.

1787
02:44:06.405 --> 02:44:07.420
Exile.

1788
02:44:15.653 --> 02:44:17.303
Telemachus will be king.

1789
02:44:21.100 --> 02:44:25.421
We'll go west together. into the unknown.
so that I may honor my men.

1790
02:44:28.833 --> 02:44:32.672
Build the fastest ship.
gather the best

1791
02:44:33.461 --> 02:44:35.431
Gather your crew and sail toward the horizon.

1792
02:44:36.440 --> 02:44:38.759
We'll chase the setting sun.

1793
02:44:41.053 --> 02:44:43.511
Why will those stories only be sung?

1794
02:44:45.999 --> 02:44:48.127
Because songs will be the only thing
they'll have

1795
02:44:48.230 --> 02:44:49.806
In memory of those
who knew how to write.

1796
02:44:52.242 --> 02:44:53.968
One war...

1797
02:44:54.339 --> 02:44:56.533
Civilization will be reborn.

1798
02:44:57.901 --> 02:44:59.419
One man...

1799
02:45:02.867 --> 02:45:05.662
And a new dawn will break over the dark world.

1800
02:45:09.207 --> 02:45:11.781
And once again. our mistakes will be forgotten.

1801
02:45:11.864 --> 02:45:28.164
Translated into Slovak by: .::Ojvih777::.

1802
02:45:28.247 --> 02:45:32.447
www.Opensubtitles.org
